\par\par \b \uc2 UK MARKET TALK ROUNDUP: SHARES LOSING\b0\par\par\uc2 2,655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2011\par 13:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stocks on the fall in the UK today. Compiled by Dow Jones Newswires Markets Desk, markets.eu@dowjones.com \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1117 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  slashes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Premier Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PFD.LN) price target to 8p from 25p following the company's profit warning. Cuts FY Ebita forecast to GBP200M from GBP231M. Describes 3Q trading as "poor". Says, the group now looks set to breach its banking covenants. Says, "with all the cash generated paying the banks, equity investors have little to look forward to until profits are stabilized and disposals are made. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Premier Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  looks in a weak position with both the banks and any would-be buyers of its businesses." Maintains neutral recommendation. Shares are down 37% at 6.3p, and the stock is the biggest faller amongst FTSE mid-caps. (andrea.tryphonides@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1115 GMT [Dow Jones] Talvivaara Mining (TLV1H.HE) shares are down 21% at 199p, after the company said a shift in focus may impact production, and as its CEO steps down. "The unfolding disaster continues," says \uc2 Numis Securities\uc2 , as 3Q production comes in well below the target. Notes cash was EUR38 million at the end of 3Q and although the company has secured further credit to the tune of EUR110 million, questions are inevitably raised regarding the need for additional funds if production is not resolved soon. Meanwhile, Liberum Capital says given the production disappointment, a change of leadership is a good thing. Liberum and Numis both have Talvivaara at buy. (michele.maatouk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1018 GMT [Dow Jones] FTSE 100 consolidates lower towards 5164 after rejecting a fresh two-week high at 5315.34 on Friday. Consolidation of this week's 4868.60/5315.34 rally will look to keep the 5072.00/5095.00 support area intact, representing the 50% Fibonacci retracement level and former intraday range highs that have reverted to support. Failure to keep this support area intact would question the validity of the recovery, although technically, only a break below 4974.00 would bring Tuesday's 4868.60 low back into focus. A push above 5315.34 would bring the September 1 reaction high at 5449.67 back into the picture, creating longer-term scope to 5527.00. The FTSE 100 is at 5271.45. (francis.bray@dowjones.com)(This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  cuts its target price for \uc2 Rentokil Initial\uc2  (RTO.LN) to 74p from 85p, to reflect the incorporation of lower peer and market multiples in its valuation model. Citi is seeing multiple contractions across the industry due to increasingly difficult market conditions, against the backdrop of a deteriorating macro environment. "Given entrenched competition and pricing pressures, management remains keen to pursue cost synergies via industry consolidation, but increasingly this feels a medium share-price driver, not near term," it notes. Citi has the stock rated at hold. Shares are down 0.6% at 67p.(michele.maatouk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] The decision by Moody's Investors Service to downgrade its ratings of 12 UK financial institutions has some effect on investor sentiment, but it does not truly reflect the banks' core businesses, according to a London-based trader. "In the long term, it's not too bad for the banks," says the trader, adding that their exposure to European sovereign debt is not that substantial. Among UK banking stocks, only \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lloyds Banking Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LLOY.LN) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RBS.LN) are in negative territory, down 2.3% and 1.6%, respectively.(toby.anderson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's target to cut its fiscal deficit to 4.7% of GDP in 2012 vs 5.4% in 2011 "appears to be too optimistic" as the global economic outlook is weakening and the nation may call elections soon, DBS Bank economist Irvin Seah says after government publishes annual report on economy. "Given that elections are coming up and the global economic outlook is worsening, fiscal policy will have to be expansionary," Seah says, adding that the government revenue is unlikely to pick up significantly in the current economic climate. Malaysia expects GDP to grow 5.0%-5.5% this year, down from the 5.0%-6.0% growth it predicted a year ago and the 7.2% expansion recorded in 2010. For 2012, it expects the economy to grow 5.0%-6.0%. (xgaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] UK bank CDS press tighter after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrades as early reports had touted larger downgrades than were actually dished out by the ratings company, a trader of financial CDS says. "It was fewer notches than what was insinuated, so we we're a bit tighter on the back of it," he says. Markit notes the five-year CDS of RBS (RBS) is 3 bps tighter at 360 bps, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BCS) is 1 bp tighter at 216 bps and \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HBC) is 3 bps tighter at 158 bps. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] TRY is up 0.7% on the day against the dollar, quoted at 1.8340, extending its early gains, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced that it plans to sell as much as $750 million at its daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  auction. Meanwhile, the yield on May 2013 bonds falls to 8.33% from 8.37% in early Friday. (yeliz.candemir@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0815 GMT [Dow Jones] The assessment by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  that the UK government will reduce its support of the banking sector might be premature, as the wider sovereign-debt and banking crises in Europe are still raging, Daiwa says. "One could argue that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  has pulled the trigger a little early in its assessment that governments will materially reduce the level of extraordinary support on offer to their banking systems, with the escalation of the sovereign and banking crisis in Europe almost certainly about to result in a broad government-backed recapitalisation of the continent's banks," says Michael Symonds, financials credit analyst at Daiwa. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgraded the ratings of 12 UK financial institutions Friday, a move that was very well-flagged and came as no surprise, Symonds notes.(art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0813 GMT [Dow Jones] ECB plan to shore up bks insufficient says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the sentiment boost will be short lived. Argues that "European bank assets will suffer further impairment until the sovereign crisis is stemmed", which "requires a more significant write down of Greek debt and a greater degree of regional fiscal unity." France is pushing for a euro-zone effort to bail out the banks because it's facing a large recapitalization bill and is "the most vulnerable to losing its AAA rating" says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/PLN may drop towards 4.35 Friday, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank BPH\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as the U.S. nonfarm payrolls data, due at 1230 GMT, could prove better than expected after Wednesday's positive ADP data. Given improved sentiment in recent days, EUR/PLN could fall towards 4.35, the bank says. EUR/PLN is now at 4.3756 from 4.3762 early Thursday. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0804 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/PLN fair value is in 4.25-4.35 range says Danske with the latter number, 4.35, reflecting Danske's 12 month forecast. That makes the recent sell-off of the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "a rather opportune timing for longer term horizon zloty longs," the bank says. In the zloty's favor: Poland has 60% more foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves than in late-2008 to deal with any significant zloty depreciation. Also, Danske says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , selling euros and buying zloty when the EUR/PLN edges above 4.50. EUR/PLN now at 4.3764 from 4.3762 early Thursday. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0804 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary output rise is set to stay muted, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  says, given worsening PMIs in major economies, stagnating new export orders and stalled domestic demand. Hungary's August adjusted industrial output contracted by 0.4% on the year, data Thursday showed. The coming months don't hold too much to be optimistic about, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Tibor Nagy says, with the sole exception of passenger car manufacturing, where ongoing investments are scheduled to turn productive by the end of 2011 and early 2012. Sees annual growth around 5% in 2011 versus August's year-to-date reading of 6.7%.(veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0802 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa says the BOJ needs to pay particular attention to the downside risks to the Japanese economy stemming from sharp moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as well as European sovereign debt problems. These comments are "a message that the BOJ stands ready to act if European sovereign debt problems worsen and lead to an even stronger yen," says Dai-ichi Life Research Institute chief economist Hideo Kumano. "My impression is that the BOJ doesn't like sharp moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, and it can draw a sword at anytime if it becomes necessary rather than remaining in wait-and-see mood," he says. If the BOJ does take steps, "it would be by beefing up asset-buying programs, though at this point it's hard to say by how much." (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 07-10-11 1118GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's total borrowings for 2011 are expected to rise significantly to MYR96.6 billion, from MYR65 billion in 2010, because of higher redemptions, loan repayments and deficit financing requirements, the government says Friday in its annual report on the economy. About MYR90.2 billion will be domestic borrowings, while MYR6.4 billion will come from external sources. Much of the borrowing details have been priced in by the bond market and shouldn't have any notable impact. The government says MYR51.0 billion will be used to repay existing debt, while the remaining balance will finance the deficit. It expects to raise MYR54.2 billion via the issuance of Malaysian government securities, or MGS, in 2011, up from MYR37.1 billion a year earlier. The government also expects to raise MYR36.0 billion from government investment issues, or Islamic bonds, this year compared with MYR24.0 billion in 2010. As of end-June 2011, the largest holders of the government's conventional and Islamic bonds were state-owned pension fund the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Employees Provident Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  (33.4%), banking institutions (19.7%), foreign holders (25.0%) and insurance companies (6.5%). (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia will continue to maintain its subsidy program for now, "albeit in a more targeted manner," to mitigate the impact of rising consumer prices on living standards, the government says Friday in its annual report on the economy. The report says domestic inflation is increasingly influenced by external factors, including supply constraints; as a result, "sustaining a low inflation environment domestically is more challenging than in the past and requires innovative measures." The government has said it remains committed to gradually reducing subsidies to bring the fiscal deficit down to 3%-4% of GDP by 2015. Malaysia forecasts a fiscal deficit of 4.7% of GDP in 2012, narrower than economists' expectations of 5.0% and down from an expected 5.4% this year. Economists say containing the budget deficit will be hard, especially if the global economy slows further, which will hurt oil prices. This will mean the subsidies can't be maintained for too long. Majority of the government's revenue comes from oil and petroleum related products. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0825 GMT [Dow Jones] The decision by Moody's Investors Service to downgrade its ratings of 12 UK financial institutions has some effect on investor sentiment, but it does not truly reflect the banks' core businesses, according to a London-based trader. "In the long term, it's not too bad for the banks," says the trader, adding that their exposure to European sovereign debt is not that substantial. Among UK banking stocks, only \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lloyds Banking Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LLOY.LN) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RBS.LN) are in negative territory, down 2.3% and 1.6%, respectively.(toby.anderson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0744 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Premier Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PFD.LN) shares are down 36% at 6p, topping the FTSE 250 fallers board, after the company's profit warning. Investec Securities expects to see FY '11 consensus EBIT falling below GBP200 million, which would be a 10%+ downgrade. "Now the question is one of whether this company can survive," says Investec. It says relatively firm statements are being made on the progress of discussions with bankers, but the equity is best avoided this morning. "To say that all this adds up to a baptism of fire for new CEO Michael Clarke feels like the understatement of the year." Has its hold rating and 15p price target under review. (michele.maatouk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] UK bank stocks are under pressure, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lloyds Banking Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LLOY.LN) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2  (RBS.LN) the two biggest decliners on the FTSE 100 index, down 2.8% and 2.5%, following \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrade of 12 UK financial institutions. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BARC.LN) is down 1.9%. Lloyds is downgraded one notch to A1 from Aa3, while RBS is cut two notches to A2 from Aa3. Oriel Securities says the downgrade had been well-flagged, reflecting the removal of government support through guaranteed liquidity schemes and the low probability of future tax-payer bailouts. However, a trader says the downgrades had been "expected, but not to that degree." He adds "the news comes at a delicate time because the banks had begun to outperform the market and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  closed above its 50-day moving average Thursday." (michele.maatouk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vedanta Resources\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VED.LN) fiscal 2Q output results were weaker-than-expected, says a London-based analyst. He notes the second quarter results are typically weaker due to monsoon season but says iron ore production is lower than his expectations, even taking into consideration the Karnataka export ban. Zinc and copper are up but Zambia copper is again a "perennial disappointment." Aluminum is also down, as previously expected, due to a pot outage at a smelter. "On the whole it's a weak quarter," he says. He expects the company's shares to underperform the market Friday based on the report. Vedanta's shares down 0.5% at 1107p. (alex.macdonald@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0658 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Premier Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' (PFD.LN) profit warning is "not the sort of continuity that we were looking for from the profit warning-littered prior regime, especially after the somewhat calamitous summertime warning," says Shore Capital. The company's 3Q results highlight not just the structural constraints on the business through its pension liabilities and indebtedness, but also seemingly systemic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues, particularly versus its peers. New CEO Michael Clarke "has his work cut out," says Shore Capital. Shore rates \f28 \uc2 \f2 Premier Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  stock at sell. Shares closed Thursday at 10p.(michele.maatouk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9288 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 07-10-11 1118GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1112   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111007e7a7000su\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
