\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 4-Zloty leads  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  bounce, more  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,084 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2011\par 17:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates after Polish cbank comments)\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds rates, may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to buoy zloty\par \par \uc2 * Improved global sentiment lifts stocks, buoys \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Hungary cbank minutes come out, eyes on weak forint\par \par \uc2 By Marcin Goettig and Jason Hovet\par \par \uc2 WARSAW/PRAGUE, Oct 5 (Reuters) - The zloty bounced to a one-week high on Wednesday after the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left open the possibility of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding to gains as emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered more from multi-month lows.\par \par \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Marek Belka said the zloty, which had recently traded at or around its lowest since June 2009, was "detached from fundamentals" and said the bank could step into the market again as it has done three times in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 The bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped lift the zloty 3.5 percent since it hit a trough at 4.537 per euro on Sept. 22.\par \par \uc2 The zloty and Hungarian forint have been the hardest hit of the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors have retreated from emerging European assets on concerns over a weakening global economy and the spiralling debt crisis in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 The selloff in the region has turned the focus towards policymakers' responses. Romania has also moved to prop up the leu, while Hungary may be forced into emergency rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 In Poland, polls have shown the possibility of a close result in a weekend election, adding to market nerves. Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk's Civic Platform (PO) party looked set to win, according to the latest poll.\par \par \uc2 Poland's Monetary Policy Council (MPC) left its key rate on hold at 4.50 percent on Wednesday and said it would take a wait-and-see approach with a tendency towards tightening.\par \par \uc2 "I was slightly surprised by how relatively hawkish Belka was. It seems to me that the (MPC) is trying to send a slightly more hawkish signal than they fundamentally believe in to give some support to the zloty," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 's Lars Christensen said.\par \par \uc2 By 1459 GMT, the zloty firmed 0.7 percent to 4.381 to the euro. Hungary's forint , after see-sawing around a 29-month low at the psychological level of 300 to the euro, rose 0.9 percent to 297.16.\par \par \uc2 The forint has lost 7.9 percent since Sept. 1, while the zloty is down 5.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "We think that EUR/PLN will be capped below 4.45 for the time being. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and finance ministry have shown strong commitment to prevent PLN weakness beyond this level and we expect more \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  said in a client note earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 SAME BOAT FOR FORINT?\par \par \uc2 Similarly in Hungary, the forint's drop has fuelled speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may undertake emergency rate hikes as it did in the 2008-09 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Minutes from a September policy meeting showed Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Julia Kiraly tabled a proposal for a 25 basis point rise, the first proposal for monetary tightening since January.\par \par \uc2 But dealers said overall the minutes were not overly hawkish. "It's still a question what (market developments) could scare the central bankers enough to trigger a rate hike," a Budapest fixed income trader said.\par \par \uc2 "The forint/zloty has fallen apart... The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and there is a strengthening group of hedge funds who now play against (forint assets)."\par \par \uc2 Hungary bond yields fell up to 30 basis points in volatile trade, with more swings likely.\par \par \uc2 Central European stock markets, which have lost 11-16 percent since Sept. 1 and are down up to 30 percent in 2011, rebounded on Wednesday, led by Budapest's 3.8 percent rise.\par \par \uc2 The region was supported by gains in global stocks following a European pledge to shore up banks against the euro zone debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  placed seven Hungarian banks on review for a possible downgrade. It cited a recently approved law allowing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgage holders to repay their debt at a big discount to market exchange rates.\par \par \uc2 With the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the only in the region likely to tolerate a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  thanks to low inflation, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  recommended buying the euro versus the Czech crown on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The crown -- seen as the safest central European investment and the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold its 2011 gains -- added 0.8 percent to 24.724, while the Romanian leu inched up 0.1 percent to 4.313 to the euro.\par \par \uc2 The EUR/RON rate is still above the 4.3 level at which dealers are on alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Romanian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold euros through third parties last week, helping the leu rebound from a 15-month low of 4.3725. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par                                             today    in 2011 \par Czech crown        24.724   24.915  +0.77%    +1.12% \par Polish zloty        4.381    4.41   +0.66%    -9.66%\par Hungarian forint  297.16   299.8    +0.89%    -6.45%\par Croatian kuna       7.5      7.508  +0.11%    -1.6%\par Romanian leu        4.313    4.316  +0.07%    -1.85%\par Serbian dinar     101.61   101.74   +0.13%    +4.25%\par Yield Spreads\par Czech treasury bonds <0#CZBMK=>\par 2-yr T-bond   CZ2YT=RR   +40 basis points to  108bps over bmk*\par 7-yr T-bond   CZ7YT=RR    -7 basis points to  +124bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond   CZ9YT=RR    -9 basis points to  +135bps over bmk*\par Hungarian treasury bonds <0#HUBMK=>\par 3-yr T-bond   HU3YT=RR    -22 basis points to  +669bps over bmk*\par 5-yr T-bond   HU5YT=RR    -17 basis points to  +679bps over bmk*\par 10-yr T-bond   HU10YT=RR  -33 basis points to  +641bps over bmk*\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *Benchmark is German bond equivalent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All data taken from Reuters at 1704 CET.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  percent change calculated from the daily domestic\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 close at 1600 GMT. For related news and prices, click on the codes in brackets: All emerging market news Spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates Eastern Europe spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Middle East spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2