\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Poland keeps rates flat, warily eyes weaker zloty\b0\par\par\uc2 577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2011\par 13:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * C.bank holds fire on rates, sees CPI high for now\par \par \uc2 * Frets over weaker zloty, says more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  possible\par \par \uc2 * Analysts expect rate cuts next year\par \par \uc2 (Recasts with news conference, statement, analyst)\par \par \uc2 By Piotr Bujnicki\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Oct 5 - Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept borrowing costs unchanged on Wednesday and signalled there was unlikely to be any early change as it tries to balance the inflationary risks of a weakening zloty with a looming economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 Governor Marek Belka also reiterated the bank's readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in support of the zloty as its level did not reflect Poland's healthy economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The bank left the main rate at 4.50 percent, as forecast by all 23 analysts polled earlier by Reuters. Polish headline inflation was 4.3 percent in August, well above the bank's 2.5 percent target but off a May peak of 5.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 "In the coming months, annual CPI inflation will continue to run at an elevated level, primarily due to the previously observed sharp rise in global commodity prices," the bank's Monetary Policy Council (MPC) said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 "In the medium term, inflation will be curbed by the anticipated decline in domestic economic growth amidst fiscal tightening," it said, adding that CPI should then return to target.\par \par \uc2 But the MPC, which has been worried by an 11 percentage point fall in the zloty against the euro in the third quarter, also said it did not rule out further hikes.\par \par \uc2 The Polish authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times since Sept. 23 to buy zlotys for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a flight of investors from emerging markets due to the deepening euro zone crisis and fears of a second global recession.\par \par \uc2 "Further such actions (\b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) are possible ... The zloty is detached from (\b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic) fundamentals," Belka told a news conference after the rate decision.\par \par \uc2 Poland's Finance Minister Jacek Rostowski said in comments elsewhere that he supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that the ministry will exchange its EU funds on the market from "time to time" at prices it deems "favourable".\par \par \uc2 ZLOTY\par \par \uc2 The zloty gained 0.2 percent after these comments but surrendered the gains after Belka added: "Everything is dependent on the global situation. We do not intend to fight with the (prevailing) tendencies."\par \par \uc2 "We do not have a programme of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those that took place (...) were aimed at calming the markets. The anti-inflation character of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is clear," he added.\par \par \uc2 The zloty traded at 4.3825 by 1500 GMT, 0.7 percent up on the day thanks to improved sentiment on global markets.\par \par \uc2 "Overall, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bias looks to have been maintained with confirmation... that rates are more likely to be raised than cut in the next move," said Peter Attard Montalto of \uc2 Nomura International\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "But it is rather hard to see the triggers for the next hike till there is actual evidence of (zloty weakening) passing through to CPI, which may not come until the first quarter (of 2012) when there would be falling inflation anyway."\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the MPC to start cutting interest rates next year given the expected economic slowdown, with the timing of the first move depending, among other factors, on the zloty exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Marcin Goettig; Writing by Gareth Jones; Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-RATES/ (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111008e7a5000ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
