\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Slovak Showdown Over EFSF On Agenda\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leos Rousek   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 05:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par    Local/GMT \par  \par      all day Poland \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate setters start two-day policy meeting \par    1130/0930 Hungary 3-month t-bill auction \par    1700/1500 Slovak coalition government leaders meet on EFSF \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Leaders in Slovakia's four-party coalition government plan to meet late Tuesday in a last-ditch effort to overcome divisions that could derail the expanded euro-zone bailout fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Slovakia has yet to ratify the euro-zone agreement to expand the EUR440 billion fund. All 17 euro countries must ratify the accord, reached at a July summit, for it to take effect.\par \par \uc2 In Poland, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate-setting council begins a two-day meeting that is expected to deliver a "no change" decision Wednesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg    Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT           High    Low     12/31 \par EUR/CZK 24.852-908 24.812-89 +0.12   24.869   24.869  -0.69 \par EUR/HUF 298.52-9.24 297.62-8.33 +0.30 298.56  297.64  +7.41 \par EUR/PLN 4.4097-180 4.4131-221 -0.08 4.4186   4.4108 +11.55 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par          Hungary        Poland          Czech Republic \par        Mon    Fri     Mon     Fri       Mon     Fri \par 3 Yrs 7.25%  7.23%   4.46%   4.45%(1)  1.81%   1.84%(2) \par 5 yrs 7.70%  7.73%   5.30%   5.27%     2.00%   2.02% \par 10yrs 8.25%  8.18%   5.93%   5.91%     3.19%   3.23% \par (1) 2Yrs (2) 4Yrs \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par EU Enlarged 15           1,704.53 loss  25.11 dn 1.45% \par WIG 20                   2,146.56 loss  42.17 dn 1.93% \par BUX                      15,509.70 loss 265.42 dn 1.68% \par PX                       917.60   loss  16.30 dn 1.75% \par  \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 POLAND\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the zloty for the third time in three trading days Monday, sending a strong message to the market that it remains committed to preventing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The Czech government budget deficit widened in the three quarters through end-September from the corresponding period a year earlier due to lower tax collection and higher expenditure, data released Monday by the Finance Ministry showed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: Slovak Finance Minister Ivan Miklos said early Tuesday he won't recommend Slovakia seek collateral for a new round of lending to Greece. Miklos made the comments following a meeting of euro-zone finance ministers in Luxembourg.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's parliament on Monday adopted a new energy strategy that aims to reduce the country's dependence on energy imports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Budapest threatened to pull its support for Belgrade's bid to join the European Union over a Serbian law it says discriminates against the Hungarian minority, the MTI news agency said Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungarian prosecutors have criminally charged former prime minister Ferenc Gyurcsany in a corruption case Monday, state news agency MTI reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Romanian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose to EUR33.62 billion in September from EUR32.55 billion a month earlier, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data showed Monday, news agency Mediafax reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leos Rousek, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 315 290; leos.rousek@dowjones.com (Marynia Kruk in Warsaw and Veronika Gulyas in Budapest also contributed to this briefing.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe}}}\uc2 \f2  for the new Dow Jones blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region. [ 10-03-11 2310ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020111004e7a40000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
