\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Global Mood,German-Slovak Talks In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leos Rousek   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2011\par 05:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par    Local/GMT \par  \par    0900/0700 Czech September business confidence \par     all day  Germany President visits Slovakia \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment on core European and global markets will drive regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stocks Monday and traders don't rule out further selloffs of equities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In Bratislava, German President Christian Wulff is scheduled to meet Slovak government officials, including parliament's speaker Richard Sulik, who leads the group of renegade lawmakers opposing Slovakia's endorsement of the European Financial Stability Facility. Instead, Sulik and his party colleagues advocate an orderly bankruptcy of Greece to ring fence the rest of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  union from its insolvent southern member.\par \par \uc2 In Prague, investors may get some sense of the economy's prospects from the September's business confidence data release. The index has been in decline since the spring amid the growing pessimism among managers over the export-driven economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                    2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 24.631-705 24.614-704  +0.04 24.687   24.630  -1.53 \par EUR/HUF 288.52-90.76 288.88-90.22 +0.03 289.67 287.56 +4.09 \par EUR/PLN 4.3679-878 4.3659-794 +0.12 4.3858   4.3644  +10.64 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par          Hungary        Poland          Czech Republic \par        Fri    Thu     Fri     Thu       Fri     Thu \par 3 Yrs 6.83%  6.70%   4.49%   4.48%(1)  1.73%   1.78%(2) \par 5 yrs 7.50%  7.35%   5.25%   5.28%     1.92%   1.92% \par 10yrs 8.25%  8.15%   6.98%   6.07%     3.14%   3.05% \par (1) 2Yrs (2) 4Yrs \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par EU Enlarged 15       1,646.66 loss     1.33    dn 0.08% \par WIG 20               2,101.23 loss     21.32   dn 1.00% \par PX                    864.1   loss     31.8    dn  3.6% \par BUX                  14,940.80 loss    211.77  dn 1.40% \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 POLAND\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to boost the zloty Friday was designed to increase risks for those market participants who bet the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will weaken, and a new \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is possible at any time, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor told the state-owned Polish Radio.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Hungary's Competition Office on Friday said it cleared the acquisition of dairy company Mlekarna Hlinsko by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Agrofert\f28 \uc2 \f2 . GVH reviewed the acquisition because the companies, both based in the Czech Republic, share markets for several dairy products in Hungary. The office found the companies' combined market share would not be significant after the merger.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's corporate lending is likely to drop further because of slower economic growth and bank's reduced ability to lend, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the recent past, the main obstacle to lending was banks' desire to avoid risks\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: Reducing cyanide levels in the tailings pond of a future Romanian gold mine would cost "several dozen million dollars," a manager of the Canadian company seeking to exploit it told AFP. Rosia Montana Gold Corp., which is 80% held by Canadian firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gabriel Resources Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (GBU.T), wants to use cyanide to extract 300 metric tons of gold from an open-cast mine in Rosia Montana, a picturesque Carpathian mountain village.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Bulgaria threatened Saturday to make European Union decision-making more difficult after having its entry into the passport-free Schengen travel area blocked.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leos Rousek, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 315 290; leos.rousek@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe}}}\uc2 \f2  for the new Dow Jones blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region. [ 09-25-11 2312ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110926e79q00007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
