\par\par \b \uc2 Poland To Deter Some Investors From Betting Against Zloty\b0\par\par\uc2 By Marcin Sobczyk   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2011\par 16:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week will deter some investors from betting that the zloty will weaken, while stabilizing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term, economists said.\par \par \uc2 The market may test the resolve of Polish authorities to defend the zloty if turmoil increases, they added.\par \par \uc2 The Polish government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  propped up the zloty Friday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been weakening since July as investors exited emerging markets due to concerns over the euro zone's sovereign debt and worries about the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose nearly 3% against the euro in less than 30 minutes Friday afternoon. At 1451 GMT Monday, it remained close to these levels. It traded at PLN4.4181 to the euro, nearly 1% weaker on the day and nearly 7% lower than on Sept. 1.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have a particular target exchange rate, and the purpose of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to increase risk for those investors who bet the zloty will weaken further, said Marek Belka, governor of the National Bank of Poland, in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Belka said last week the zloty was undervalued.\par \par \uc2 The Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was successful, knocking the euro rate down from PLN4.52 to PLN4.36, BRE Bank said. It allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government to distort the speculative game on the zloty at a relatively low cost, possibly EUR1 billion-EUR2 billion, it added.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, but it will likely do so at a different rate than EUR/PLN4.50, BPH said.\par \par \uc2 Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved the zloty away from a level that would inflate Poland's external debt, and thus its overall public debt above 55% of economic output.\par \par \uc2 According to economists, the level that would push debt above this level--triggering austerity measures, according to Polish law--is between EUR/PLN4.60 and EUR/PLN4.80. The debt measurements are taken at the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 The risk of a fresh \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now low, said Invest-Bank. The zloty will likely stabilize and investors will be made less eager to bet that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will weaken, it added.\par \par \uc2 If Greece defaults on its debt and markets turn even more averse to holding emerging market assets, investors will be eager to test the resolve of the Polish authorities, which may find it difficult to reverse the negative trend on the zloty, Citi economist Cezary Chrapek said.\par \par \uc2 -By Marcin Sobczyk, Dow Jones Newswires; +48 22 447 2432; marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com [ 26-09-11 1443GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110926e79q000zq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
