\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Polish PM: Constant  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Out Of The Question\b0\par\par\uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 14:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--Constant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is "out of the question," Polish Prime Minister Donald Tusk was quoted as saying by PAP state news agency Friday, after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened more than 5% over the past seven days.\par \par \uc2 "For us, it's key that this storm not destabilize our financial situation," Tusk said. "I say we are well-prepared, constant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on financial markets is out of the question."\par \par \uc2 The zloty is now trading around 4.51 against the euro, compared with 4.29 last Friday. It has weakened about 11% over the past two months as investors have become more risk-averse and are fleeing emerging markets in response to euro-zone sovereign debt concerns.\par \par \uc2 "Poland, as a country, should not take up the speculation game on a large scale," Tusk said. "We will use our existing tools, meaning common sense and moderate [tools], and will gather supplies in case something really tips over."\par \par \uc2 Economists increasingly expect Poland's Finance Ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action to defend the zloty, rather than relying on the verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the two institutions have engaged in since early September.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a self-imposed 55% threshold of debt-to-gross domestic product, the crossing of which would trigger an automatic tightening of fiscal policy. A weaker zloty on the last day of the year could increase the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, potentially causing a breach of this threshold.\par \par \uc2 Hence, a continued "disorderly" weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from now until the end of the year is the last thing the Polish government wants, economists said, especially as the country is due for a parliamentary election Oct. 9. The ruling Civic Platform party is hoping to become the first party to be re-elected for a second term since 1989.\par \par \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Marek Belka said Thursday the weakened zloty was making it more difficult for the bank to keep inflation in check, while the volatility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the uncertainty this creates could curb investment in the Polish economy.\par \par \uc2 "We're observing the scale of events, [and] what is most worrying is that the source of the turmoil is outside Poland," Tusk said. "Speculation on the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been an undisputed fact for the last few days."\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, Polish Finance Minister Jacek Rostowski said Poland's public debt remains safely below 55% of economic output, the level that would trigger austerity measures, despite the recent weakening of the zloty.\par \par \uc2 "We're quite far from the exchange rate that would push debt above 55% of gross domestic product at the end of the year," he said on television channel TVN CNBC.\par \par \uc2 -By Marynia Kruk, Dow Jones Newswires; +48 22 447-2431; marynia.kruk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 23, 2011 08:09 ET (12:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110923e79n0037s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
