\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Polish Government,  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Boost Zloty\b0\par\par\uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 17:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- Polish authorities jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  after growing tired of weak zloty\par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosts zloty by nearly 3% in less than 30 minutes\par \par \uc2 -- Zloty dragged away from levels that could trigger austerity measures\par \par \uc2 -- Prime minister says \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't "constantly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 " \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Marcin Sobczyk \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--The Polish government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Friday to boost the zloty, pushing it away from a level that would trigger austerity measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before Friday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the zloty had been weakening since July as investors exited emerging markets over euro-zone sovereign debt concerns and worries about the global economy. A weak zloty inflates the country's debts denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold an undisclosed amount of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy the zloty, it said in a statement. The government appeared to be back in the spot market Friday also selling euros, traders said. Both moves came after a series of unsuccessful government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent days that showed their discomfort with the weak zloty.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The zloty rose nearly 3% against the euro in less than 30 minutes Friday afternoon when Polish state-owned Bank Gospodarstwa Krajowego and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . At 1332 GMT, the zloty traded at PLN4.38 from PLN4.52 earlier in the day. That put the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back at Wednesday's levels, which were still about 6% below the exchange rate of Sept. 1.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before the zloty began weakening, Poland's public debt was around 53% of gross domestic product, well below the European Union average. But as the zloty weakens, debt swells. And if it crosses the crucial 55% threshold at the end of the year, Polish law requires the government to balance the budget or have a surplus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That is a situation the government would like to avoid because public discussion of the possibility of automatic austerity measures could undermine its chances of getting reelected for a second term when Poland elects its new parliament on Oct. 9.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's debt is still at a safe distance from the 55% threshold, Finance Minister Jan Vincent-Rostowski said late Thursday, and the country has three months until debt measurements are taken, in line with the domestic methodology that excludes certain categories of debt and produces a result about two percentage points lower than what is reported to the European Commission. He and Marek Belka, governor of the National Bank of Poland, said Thursday the zloty is very much undervalued and its exchange rate no longer reflects the fundamentals of the Polish economy, which they called "healthy." The Polish economy grew 4.3% on the year in the second quarter of 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But economists said a zloty at pre-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels leaves little wiggle room for the finance ministry, which has regularly been selling its euros in the spot market since April. Public debt will be above 55% of GDP if the zloty weakens to around PLN4.66, according to BRE Bank, or PLN4.70, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government is likely to sell more euros in the coming days and weeks to raise zlotys that are paid to farmers, said Rafal Benecki, economist at ING. In his view, public debt will cross the 55% threshold with EUR/PLN at 4.60.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Marcin Sobczyk, Dow Jones Newswires; +48 22 447 2432; marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 23, 2011 11:24 ET (15:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110923e79n004ks\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
