\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT:  \highlight22\uc2 Poland intervention \highlight\uc2  removes PLN underperformance, says ING\b0\par\par\uc2 188 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 17:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 EUR/PLN fell to 4.40 from 4.51 following the moves Friday by the state-owned bank BGK and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy zloty. Polish public debt may exceed 55% of GDP if EUR/PLN reaches 4.60, notes ING. This is a crucial threshold as the budget will have be balanced or have a surplus if it is crossed by the end of the year. And Friday's moves seem "to confirm the [Finance Ministry, which is a client of BGK] and [the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] stepped into the market before that level," says Rafal Benecki, senior economist at ING. BGK was also buying zloty-denominated government debt, he adds. EUR/PLN had overshot the recent EUR/USD slide and weakened more than other central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so "the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  removed the majority of the [zloty's] underperformance," he says. He expects the finance ministry to appear in the market again in coming days, as it has EUR3.4 billion from the EU for direct payment to farmers. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com) [ 23-09-11 1503GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110923e79n000zn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
