\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-Polish zloty fall temporary-finance minister\b0\par\par\uc2 373 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2011\par 18:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Says zloty's decline result of hysteria\par \par \uc2 * Sees Poland weathering crisis well\par \par \uc2 * Convinced Poland will meet 2011 4 pct GDP growth target\par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Lidia Kelly\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - The recent weakening of the Polish zloty is of a temporary and panicky nature, Finance Minister Jacek Rostowski told Reuters on Friday, adding that the country's economy remains on track for its targeted 4 percent growth in gross domestic product terms.\par \par \uc2 "Absolutely, it is temporary," Rostowski said in an interview on the sidelines of the International Monetary Fund/World Bank fall meeting in Washington, when asked about the zloty's fall.\par \par \uc2 "It reminds me of hysteria from the first quarter of 2009 ... The panic then was even stronger but it only affected countries of our region, and now it is striking also the strong industrial nations of Asia."\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Polish monetary authorities launched an unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt markets to rescue the zloty, pummelled this week by the global flight from risk [ID:nL5E7KN2TJ].\par \par \uc2 The zloty <EURPLN=> has tested new 27-months lows this week, but it has gained nearly 3 percent after the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the state-owned BGK bank. [ID:nP6E7KC04L]\par \par \uc2 Rostowski also said the zloty's fall is nothing extraordinary and tracks the decline in value of a horde of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> <MYR=MY>, Taiwanese dollar <TWD=>, or South Korea's won <KRW=KFTC>.\par \par \uc2 The Polish finance minister also said that the country's economy is weathering Europe's crisis well, fighting it off with healthy macroeconomic fundamentals, such as August's sound industrial output, retail sales and unemployment data.\par \par \uc2 "Without underestimating the turbulences, as it would be absurd to do so because they are gigantic, our economy is entering them very, very strong."\par \par \uc2 He added that, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent cautious outlook on growth, Poland should meet its official 4 percent GDP expansion for this year.\par \par \uc2 "I am not forecasting, but I do not see a reason why we should not achieve it."\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Lidia Kelly, Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-ZLOTY/ROSTOWSKI (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110923e79n0016j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
