\par\par \b \uc2 Polish Central Banker: Won't Give Markets Pleasure Of Verbal  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 13:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't going to give \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders satisfaction by being drawn into a reaction to the zloty's sharp drop Friday, as this is exactly what they want, Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Marek Belka said.\par \par \uc2 "They're waiting for it [a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ]," Belka told broadcaster TVN CNBC Friday. "I'm not going to give them this pleasure."\par \par \uc2 He added he didn't know what was driving the zloty's weakening, but that it had nothing to do with events in Poland.\par \par \uc2 "If someone is testing [us], that means they're trying to provoke us into some sort of reaction which will then be exploited in future rounds of speculative tests," Belka said. "No, no, no, we're just observing... someone is going to lose money."\par \par \uc2 -By Marynia Kruk, Dow Jones Newswires; +48 22 447-2431; marynia.kruk@dowjones.com [ 09-09-11 1147GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110909e799000x3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
