\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/Emerging Europe: Will  \highlight22\uc2 Poland Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Boost Zloty?\b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2011\par 10:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (This article has been posted on Emerging Europe, The Wall Street Journal Online's site for news and analysis on Central and Eastern Europe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope }}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Posted by Marcin Sobczyk\par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty's underperformance during the current wave of the euro-zone jitters is again leading to concern that the country's weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may inflate public debt, forcing austerity measures. Economists wonder if the state will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  anytime soon to prevent that from happening.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's public debt was around 53% of economic output at the end of last year, according to Poland's methodology. Even though debt reported to the European Union is slightly bigger due to accounting differences, both measures are at a level much below average for the euro zone or the European Union. But Poland has a system of self-imposed thresholds and a debt ceiling at 60% of gross domestic product. If it breaches 55% at the end of this year, the government would need to take radical steps to balance the budget next year, the first after its expected reelection.\par \par \uc2 The government has expected public debt to remain stable around 53% this year. Deputy Finance Minister Ludwik Kotecki said earlier this week there's no risk debt will exceed 55% of output. But economists say it may happen if the zloty is only slightly weaker than today at the end of December.\par \par \uc2 Part of Poland's public debt is denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The zloty-value of Poland's debt has gone up sharply in recent weeks as the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plummeted to a one-year low versus the euro at 4.2026 zlotys on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 According to Nomura economist Peter Attard Montalto, the moment when Poland's public debt would breach the level of 55% is 4.2363 zlotys to the euro. In his view, the government may want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the zloty right at the end of the year, as it did last December when it was already wrestling with the debt threshold.\par \par \uc2 But will they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now?\par \par \uc2 "One the one hand, they may want to keep powder dry for a year-end action, but--equally--showing strength of backstop now and preventing it from spiking higher can make the job easier at the end of the year," Montalto said, adding the finance ministry is likely to sell even more of its euros on the spot market, in line with its recent strategy, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also step in soon.\par \par \uc2 The current \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more open to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the previous one was, said Gillian Edgeworth of \uc2 Unicredit\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "That said, it's our impression at this stage that we will need to see more extreme zloty losses before this materializes," she said.\par \par \uc2 The zloty is off Thursday's one-year low, trading at 4.1358 zlotys to the euro at 1337 GMT Friday.\par \par \uc2 Relief for Polish debtors--private mortgage holders--also came Friday when the Swiss franc dropped dramatically against the euro and, consequently, to the zloty as the market speculates the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more forcefully to weaken the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The franc was almost 3% lower to the zloty Friday, at 3.76 zlotys. Less than 48 hours earlier, it was near 4.05 zlotys, a sky-high level for Poland's more than 600,000 borrowers with franc-linked loans.\par \par \uc2 The franc's strength has led to comments the quality of Polish mortgage portfolios could deteriorate and private consumption weaken as people with franc mortgages become increasingly cash-strapped. For the time being, that risk is no longer on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 15, 2011 04:17 ET (08:17 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110815e78f001p2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
