\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Poland \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bank Ready To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  When Necessary - Exec\b0\par\par\uc2 145 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2011\par 10:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should the situation in the euro zone threaten the stability of the Polish zloty, said Malgorzata Zaleska, board member at the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bank won't announce such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  beforehand, she said in an interview with broadcaster TVN CNBC.\par \par \uc2 Amid concerns over Greek sovereign debt, the zloty earlier this month weakened sharply, hitting PLN4.06 to the euro on July 12, the highest level since March.\par \par \uc2 It then recovered as euro zone leaders were negotiating a second rescue package for Greece and at 0831 GMT on Friday traded at PLN3.98 to the euro.\par \par \uc2 -By Malgorzata Halaba and Marcin Sobczyk, Dow Jones Newswires; +48 22 447 2432; marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com [ 22-07-11 0837GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110722e77m000lk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
