\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  edge up, zloty firms on BGK sales\b0\par\par\uc2 1,062 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2011\par 15:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Govt bank in Warsaw steps in to sell euros, dollars\par \par \uc2 * Bond yields drop slightly after crude falls\par \par \uc2 * Hopes that lower oil price may curb inflation\par \par \uc2 (Releads with zloty, BGK sales)\par \par \uc2 By Sandor Peto and Radu Marinas\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, May 6 (Reuters) - Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged up modestly, propped up by hopes that falling oil prices will help curb inflation, with hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales by state-owned Polish Bank BGK boosting the zloty.\par \par \uc2 A dealer said BGK - mandated to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  under a government plan to regularly convert EU funds in order to firm the zloty, keep price pressures in check and reduce the need for more rate hikes - sold euros first and then dollars.[ID:nWSF010280]\par \par \uc2 At 1110 GMT the zloty <EURPLN=> firmed 0.3 percent to 3.9475 per euro, but shed some gains afterwards to trade 0.1 percent up.\par \par \uc2 "I estimate the bank exchanged 30-40 million euros and 40 million dollars into zlotys today," the dealer said. BGK and the finance ministry declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Polish Finance Minister Jacek Rostowski confirmed the ministry had started to regularly exchange euro-denominated EU funds for zlotys. [ID:nWSF010278]\par \par \uc2 Yields on Central European government bonds and forward rate agreements edged lower after the Czech and euro zone central banks held rates the previous day and as crude prices fell.\par \par \uc2 The Czech crown <EURCZK=> and Hungarian forint <EURHUF=> both firmed 0.2 percent against the euro, to 24.108 and 265.08 respectively. The leu <EURRON=> shed 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CNB) left rates on hold on Thursday at a record low of 0.75 percent -- 50 basis points below the euro zone's key rate -- and struck a dovish tone, analysts said [ID:nLDE744185]. Market rates, which have been pricing in a rise in rates by the end of the summer, fell as a result.\par \par \uc2 Yields on the 9x12 forward rate agreement (FRA), reflecting three-month rates in nine months' time, eased 2 basis points, bringing total falls to 12 points since Thursday's opening.\par \par \uc2 Markets are now pricing in two small CNB hikes by end-2011.\par \par \uc2 "The crown should underperform in the coming months as dividend payments generally weigh in Q2 and the CNB remains dovish," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  said in a report.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian government bond yields and FRAs also dipped.\par \par \uc2 "The FRA curve is quite flat, neither a hike (in the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 6 percent base rate) nor a cut is priced in," one fixed income trader said.\par \par \uc2 "Yesterday's crude price fall has reduced inflation risks, but the market will not react strongly to the price movement of just one day," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 Commodities spiralled lower again on Friday. [ID:nL3E7G605T]\par \par \uc2 POSSIBLE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  IN \b \highlight22\uc2 POLAND\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be next in the region to decide on interest rates, on May 11. Lowered expectations of another ECB rate rise in June have decreased the perceived chances of rapid tightening in Poland and supported bond prices.\par \par \uc2 The ECB left euro zone interest rates at 1.25 percent as expected on Thursday and markets interpreted comments by the bank's President Jean-Claude Trichet as suggesting it would not hike again in June. [ID:nLDE7440GG]\par \par \uc2 However, ECB policymaker Ewald Nowotny said on Friday that financial markets had "over-interpreted" the ECB's intentions by scaling back rate-hike bets, adding: "There can be no idea about (the ECB) being dovish". [ID:nHEL010146]\par \par \uc2 Polish bond yields dropped 2-4 basis points on Friday while FRAs <PLNFRA=> are currently pricing in a 33-basis-point increase in the three-month WIBOR interbank rate over the next three months, versus 39 points at the beginning of the week.\par \par \uc2 In Budapest, one trader said: "From one minute to the other 2-year IRSs (interest rate swap) slipped to 6.21/24 from 6.27/30... There were sellers in FRAs, too."\par \par \uc2 Romania's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised its year-end inflation forecast to 5.1 percent, well above its target, and uncertain energy prices could push it higher. [ID:nLDE7440Y7]\par \par \uc2 Markets will look to comments made by the International Monetary Fund on Monday at the end of a two-week mission visit to Romania to review its new precautionary aid package. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT----------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                                            today    in 2011 \par Czech crown      <EURCZK=>   24.108    24.166  +0.24%    +3.7%\par Polish zloty     <EURPLN=>   3.953     3.957   +0.1%     +0.13%\par Hungarian forint <EURHUF=>   265.08    265.5   +0.16%    +4.87%\par Croatian kuna    <EURHRK=>   7.378     7.355   -0.31%    +0.03%\par Romanian leu     <EURRON=>   4.116     4.109   -0.17%    +2.84%\par Serbian dinar    <EURRSD=>   99.45     98.82