\par\par \b \uc2 Poland Warms Up To Stronger Zloty As Euro Rises\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cynthia Lin and Clare Connaghan   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 763 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2011\par 22:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--While most emerging markets are doggedly trying to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the zloty Thursday, viewing it as the better of two difficult options for keeping it in sync with the euro.\par \par \uc2 In announcing a plan to convert euros to zlotys in the open market, Polish authorities expressed hope that a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might substitute for some of the tightening that would otherwise be expected from monetary policy. With the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently raising rates and the euro rising sharply along with that, the risk of a relatively weak zloty has raised concerns over imported inflation.\par \par \uc2 "This is a fairly significant development," said Manik Narain, emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "They want to show the markets that they're happy to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen to allow some burden of monetary tightening to be done through the exchange rate."\par \par \uc2 The zloty exchange rate to the euro is especially important as it is the conduit through which most of Poland trades and foreign investment flows.\par \par \uc2 Though less significant than Hungary and the Czech Republic, Poland holds strong bilateral ties with the euro zone. The Polish government has hinted that they believe the zloty is still cheap, suggesting that they don't expect complaints from exporters and that a stronger zloty is needed to fend off imported inflation.\par \par \uc2 Poland's exports make up about a third of gross domestic product, whereas its Eastern European peers are almost double that. And relative to its western European neighbors, Poland runs a smaller economy that is susceptible to conditions abroad.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's announcement sent the euro diving against the zloty from PLN3.9835 to around PLN3.95, which \uc2 UBS\uc2 's Narain points out is the exact level the pair held at the start of this year. Narain added he sees fair value at PLN3.60 on the euro, and targets another 3% appreciation by the zloty.\par \par \uc2 Others are less optimistic and believe the markets have mistakenly reacted.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only an artificial form of appreciation, and without higher interest rates, the zloty will actually suffer. According to Bartosz Pawlowski, head of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , markets had priced in four 25-basis point rate increases this year, and now he sees only two.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, policymakers in Poland are hoping that a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will slow the pace of rising rates. With its domestic economy still in a tenuous state, it is feared that higher interest could cripple growth.\par \par \uc2 March unemployment remained elevated at 13.1% and wage growth has been subdued. The nation's consumer price index, however, rose far above expectations to 4.3% last month.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, tightening by the ECB may slow activity in Europe and reduce demand for Eastern European exports.\par \par \uc2 Poland also faces a heavy load of national debt, which is inching closer to the country's statutory limit of 55% of gross domestic product. As the government cracks down on fiscal spending, monetary authorities may be compelled to keep rates accommodative for the time being, while a strong zloty would attract foreign funding.\par \par \uc2 "I would suspect that the government is happy to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate to make local debt more attractive," said Ilan Solot of \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . "They have to issue a lot of debt, and good yield there will help funding efforts by the government."\par \par \uc2 As emerging economies fight off rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to protect exporters, Poland's move may be the latest sign that developing nations are becoming more willing to use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength to combat inflation.\par \par \uc2 While Poland is undoubtedly unique in its ties to the euro zone, analysts at BBH say that the nation's approach is one the "rest of emerging markets should follow," and that "if continued, the strong appreciation observed in many emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will start to trigger policy reposes."\par \par \uc2 A number of nations have already given in to higher rates--including Poland's two rate increases this year--but inflation has ticked relentlessly higher, and strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may very well be the next line of fire.\par \par \uc2 The "spot allowing to move in Brazil and the nature of the latest wave of policy measures suggests that even the Brazilians--the biggest proponents of antiappreciation--are changing," said Koon Chow, emerging market director at Barclay's Capital in London.\par \par \uc2 -By Cynthia Lin, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4403; cynthia.lin@dowjones.com [ 21-04-11 2028GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110421e74l001dy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
