\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Euro Can't Push Through $1.40\b0\par\par\uc2 1,245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2010\par 16:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1401 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD gives back some gains as it retreats further from 1.40. Pair trading flat, at 1.3917 from 1.3914 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG. Technicals driving market now, says Flavio Giust of Key Bank. "Whereas $1.20 had been measuring level back in June, now it's $1.40." Market remains reluctant to test too wide of that level currently. USD/JPY hanging in at 81.87 up from 81.74 late Tue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1400 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish treasury bonds weaken following higher-than-expected September CPI, to +2.5% from +2.0% in August. But traders expect the bonds to bounce back on Thursday as the market starts to realize rate hike in October is still unlikely. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.73% from 4.59% late Tuesday, 5-year at 5.14% from 5.04%, and 10-year at 5.52% from 5.44%. EUR/PLN is at 3.9484 from 3.9512 earlier Wednesday, USD/PLN at 2.8331 from 2.8259. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1354 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors are often obsessed by round numbers in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and there is much discussion about AUD and CAD breaking parity, says CitiFx. "We see this obsession as misplaced since both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are largely driven by global asset market and economic conditions," it says. "Bigger surprise" will be how fast parity broken under the right scenario. The "right conditions" are a continuation of the equity and commodities rally and continued USD pressure, Citi says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1352 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds fall Wednesday, weighed by hawkish comments from ECB governing council member Weber overnight and buoyant stocks. Weber said the risks from "exiting too late" from its easy monetary stance outweighed those from "exiting too early," adding it was necessary not to defer ending non-standard measures for too long. His comments appear in sharp contrast to the Fed stance, with FOMC minutes on Tuesday hinting that quantitative easing looked increasingly likely in November. "The ECB its clearly getting the message across that it is keen to withdraw non-standard liquidity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Money market rates are expected to keep drifting higher," says a London-based trader. Earlier Wednesday, Germany sold 10-year bunds at the lowest yield ever while euro zone industrial output grew a little more than forecast. December bunds are down 0.41 at 131.60 and the 10-year bund is down at 99.80 to yield 2.273%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1351 GMT [Dow Jones] Traditional asset-allocation trades are back in style for now, featuring inverse relationship of higher stock prices and lower Tsys. "We are struck by the equity market's ability to push higher on the prospects of further [Fed] accommodation while the upside in Tsy [prices] appears to be growing tired," says David Ader, head of government bond market strategy for CRT Capital Group. Ader suggests fatigue is setting in because the market is tired of pricing in an "unknown" amount of quantitative easing. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1349 GMT [Dow Jones] Emerging market bonds are stronger as stocks get a boost on solid earnings. Sentiment also strengthened after FOMC minutes a day earlier indicated the likelihood of more stimulus. China's trade data is also supporting confidence. The risk premium on \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Emerging Markets Bond Index Global is 12 bps tighter to 277 bps over Tsys. The index is up 0.33%. Brazil's benchmark bond, the Global 2040, is up 5/16 to 140 3/4 bid, according to Reuters. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1335 GMT [Dow Jones] As the yen continues to appreciate, analysts expect another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . At next week's G7 meeting, Japan will try to achieve a formal permission slip for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Adam Kritzer at forexblog.org, by arguing "'Our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't the kind of large-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that aim to achieve certain rate levels over the long term.' September's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was only 'aimed at curbing excess fluctuations' in the yen's rates." Depending on how the G7 responds (via its official statement), it may influence the likelihood of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.forexblog.org/2010/10/japan-plots-next-yen-intervention.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.forexblog.org/2010/10/japan-plots-next-yen-\b  \highlight22intervention\b0 \highlight22 .html}}}\uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1334 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's higher-than-expected September inflation rate cements expectations that the country's Monetary Policy Council will raise interest rates by 25 basis points at its October meeting, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank BPH\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. Inflation hitting the middle of the council's inflation target lends credence to the members of the council who have been calling for an increase for some time. Food prices are key determinants of inflation expectations. As they were a big contributor to September inflation, it gives the council an additional reason to raise rates. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1334 GMT [Dow Jones] Market speculation and subsequent reports that Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have moved the ruble trading band, where it allows free fluctuations, are "not reliable" in the absence of official comment, Renaissance Capital says. The ruble weakened quite a bit against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's dollar-euro trading band before recovering to close official trade Wednesday at 35.4798, compared with 35.3098 late Tuesday. Previous reports suggested the trading band was between about 34.35 and 35.35 to the basket. If the band moved, the market hasn't had the opportunity to test its parameters yet, RenCap says. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  declines to comment. Ruble little changed at 30.1205 against the dollar. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1331 GMT [Dow Jones] Short-term Eurodollar futures maintain modest bid as higher stocks and this week's Tsy auctions encourage traders to put on curve steepening trades. In this case, curve steepens as market sees short-term rates at very low levels and longer-term rates moving higher. Quarterly 2010 through 2013 Eurodollar contracts recently up 0.5 to 1.5 BP. Near-dated Dec '10 recently 0.5 BP higher at 99.68, seeing underlying 3-month dollar Libor at 0.32% when contract expires Dec. 13. Cash Libor at 0.28906%, coming in flat for 4th straight day. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1324 GMT [Dow Jones] Poland's CPI accelerated to +2.5% in September, from +2.0% August, but the Monetary Policy Council's members that are likely to support a hike are still in the minority, ING Bank says, thus sees October rather unlikely. "The continuation of the food-driven increase of inflation in the next few months could prompt the MPC to the hike in November, despite still falling core inflation and sluggish credit markets," ING says. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1324 GMT [Dow Jones] Poland's Monetary Policy Council has been rather dovish, which is why the start of a tightening cycle may not happen in October despite an uptick in CPI to 2.5% in September, says BZ WBK. It adds that CPI is likely to rise to 3% in the coming months and now would be a good moment to increase rates from their current all-time lows as Polish economic recovery continues. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com) [ 10-13-10 1004ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101013e6ad0009m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
