\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Poland's Belka Warns Against  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Devaluation To Promote Economic Growth\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2010\par 20:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON--The head of Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned against using \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations to promote economy growth, saying it is a risky strategy that does nothing to help a country's long-run prospects.\par \par \uc2 "I am worried because this destabilizes the global economy and it does not lead to rebalancing, something we all long for," Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Marek Belka said in an interview on the sidelines of annual meetings of the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 Exchange rates are a key backdrop to the weekend IMF meetings in Washington. The U.S. and Europe have repeatedly urged China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, to trade flexibly against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the yen from strengthening too much. Expectations that the Federal Reserve will take more dramatic steps to strengthen the U.S. economy, including large-scale purchases of Treasury securities known as quantitative easing, has led to a decline in the U.S. dollar, which hit an eight-month low against the euro earlier in the week. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has largely resisted quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 "If you want to strengthen your competitiveness by devaluing your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this is a sign of despair, this isn't a policy," Belka said.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Belka said the exchange rate of Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the zloty, "doesn't make my heart beat faster." But he doesn't rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to keep the zloty from climbing too high, which would weigh on exports, a key source of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth. \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April to weaken the zloty, the first such move in a decade.\par \par \uc2 "It's like in a drama, there's a gun hanging on the wall and you know that once in a while it shoots off, but you don't know when," Belka said. "The gun is there, but we have no precommitment to use it."\par \par \uc2 Belka, a former prime minister and top IMF official, joined the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June following the death of former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Slawomir Skrzypek, in a plane crash.\par \par \uc2 Belka said he worries about the global trend toward renewed use of quantitative easing to stimulate growth. The Bank of Japan on Tuesday announced a new asset purchase program. Officials at the Federal Reserve and the Bank of England have signaled they may resume asset purchases amid concerns over growth and employment.\par \par \uc2 "The American model is entrepreneurial. They have to do everything to avoid long-term structural unemployment," Belka said. "Europeans are sort of accustomed to it. Americans are not."\par \par \uc2 "This explains this drive to either fiscal stimulus or this quantitative easing too. If the exit is well built into the economy you shouldn't be so concerned; if the mop up of excess liquidity when the time comes is relatively straightforward maybe quantitative easing is not so dangerous," Belka said.\par \par \uc2 Still, the "ocean of liquidity" in the global financial system could affect Belka's efforts to keep Poland on a steady growth, low inflation path. "We have to be worried about the interest-rate disparity between Polish rates and global rates, especially European rates," Belka said. "Five years ago probably I would have thought of monetary tightening coming in earlier than in a situation like today."\par \par \uc2 Poland's economy weathered the global recession, and has recovered more quickly than much of the rest of Europe. Poland's GDP was up 3.8% in the second quarter from the previous year, almost double the rate of the European Union as a whole. Poland is in the EU but not the euro zone. The zloty depreciated during the global financial crisis, providing a "shield" for Polish exporters, Belka said.\par \par \uc2 Though euro membership remains Poland's goal, "I don't think we should drag ourselves into the euro zone now when there are still unresolved issues with countries with strained fiscal positions," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Brian Blackstone, The Wall Street Journal; brian.blackstone@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 09, 2010 14:43 ET (18:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101009e6a9000et\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
