\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Polish  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Head: "Not Sure" Country Needs Major Reforms\b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2010\par 08:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2010 02:24 EDT *DJ  Poland Belka: Downplays Growth Of Polish Public Debt\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2010 02:24 EDT *DJ  Poland Belka: Calls Govt's Planned VAT Increase "Pragmatic"\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2010 02:28 EDT *DJ  Poland Belka: Budget Deficit Should Be Around PLN30B In '11\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2010 02:29 EDT *DJ  Poland Belka: Faster Econ Recovery, Higher Budget Revenue In '11\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2010 02:30 EDT *DJ  Poland Belka: Sees '10 Budget Deficit At PLN48 Bln\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2010 02:30 EDT *DJ  Poland Belka: Zloty Isn't Presently Weak\par \par \uc2 14 Sep 2010 03:12 EDT DJ  Polish Zloty Not Weak At Present - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Head Belka\par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--The zloty isn't at a weak level at present, Marek Belka, governor of the National Bank of Poland, said in an interview with radio station Radio Zet Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 At 0638 GMT, the zloty was at 3.9302 to the euro, up from 3.9311 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 Under a previous governor, \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on April 9 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the zloty when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was around EUR/PLN3.85.\par \par \uc2 Belka also said he is "not sure" Poland needs major fiscal overhaul, as all possible austerity measures would generate savings for the budget only in the long run.\par \par \uc2 He added that the growth of Poland's debt isn't currently a problem. When asked about Poland's debt--seen growing by about $100 billion over the next four years in comparison with $50 billion of debt taken in the 1970s that eventually led to Poland defaulting in 1989--Belka said $50 billion was Poland's total annual gross domestic product in the 1970s, but only 10% of Poland's current GDP.\par \par \uc2 Belka said the government's plan for an increase in value-added taxes was a pragmatic way to tame the growth of public debt. He also said Poland should strive to lower its budget deficit to some 30 billion zlotys ($9.8 billion), which should be easy to achieve given the likely acceleration of economic recovery and higher budget revenue.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor sees Poland's budget deficit at PLN48 billion this year, compared with the official plan of PLN52.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 -By Marcin Sobczyk, Dow Jones Newswires; +48 22 447 2432; marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 14, 2010 03:12 ET (07:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100914e69e000ui\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
