\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Don't Ignore Zloty  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Risks - BoNYM\b0\par\par\uc2 1,363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2010\par 14:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1241 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is right back where it was against the dollar before \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and it's flying high against the euro too. With that in mind, it's worth paying close attention to today's comments by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Belka, says Neil Mellor at the Bank of New York Mellon in London. Belka described current PLN levels as "satisfactory" but he added that it risks a sudden climb, rather than fall, and he'd prefer any rises to be moderate. "Whether sanctioning physical \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a road that Belka is prepared to go down any time soon remains to be seen, but we strongly suspect that he will not be averse to issuing some rather more concerted verbal warnings," Mellor said. EUR/PLN trades at 3.98, stable on the day. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary's July consumer price inflation data is expected to slow sharply to 3.8% as the impact of the tax increases of 2009 drop from the year-earlier comparison, according to 10 analysts polled by Dow Jones Newswires. Several analysts expect the data to continue to highlight the depressed state of the economy, adding that some pressure on the headline figure could start to appear. Food prices will be watched closely. Data due at 0700 GMT Wednesday. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1239 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys react calmly to first bit of U.S. data, showing productivity unexpectedly fell in 2nd quarter, the 1st decline since the 4th quarter of 2008. Market focused instead on impending $24B 3-yr auction and after that the FOMC. 2-yr dn 1/32 to 0.553%, 10-yr up 4/32 to 2.813%, 30-yr up 12/32 to 3.998%. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CAD added to overnight gains Tues following Canadian housing starts data. USD was at 1.0385 after news that housing starts slipped 1.6% in July. USD was at 1.0355 just ahead of the data, and at 1.0270 late Mon. The rate of starts was 189,200 in July, down from a revised 192,300 in June. Analysts expected July housing starts of 184,000. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1233 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. productivity unexpectedly fell in the 2Q amid slower output growth and an increase in labor costs. Productivity dropped at a 0.9% vs 0.3% rise expected by economists. Companies had been using their current employees to meet increased demand, that's why profits have soared and hiring stalled. It's only one quarter, but the 2Q slowdown in productivity could be a sign that trends are shifting-good news for labor markets in the future. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1230 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  could hike benchmark rate to 2.0% by year-end from 1.50% currently to keep inflation in check amid strongly recovery economy that could expand as much as 8% this year, Deputy Governor Atchana Waiquamdee tells[Dow Jones]Newswires. She says while it could hike a further 50 bps this year, whether it tightens that much will depend on inflation data, adding the pace of future increases may slow if global economic growth falters. BOT hiked 25 basis points last month. She says so far there are no signs of "alarming" inflationary pressures in Thai economy, but adds "the pressure is building up as the economy recovers. There is a possibility that it will breach the upper part of the range, 3%, next year." She also warned that Thailand's attractiveness as destination for foreign investment risks coming under threat if country fails to deliver greater political stability following several bouts of unrest. Says domestic economy also faces potential risks from possible slowdown in global economy. See: =INTERVIEW: Bank Of Thailand: Policy Rate May Hit 2.0% This Year. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1226 GMT [Dow Jones] While analysts are split on whether the FOMC will announce an expansion of quantitative easing today, most agree using mortgage paydowns for short Treasury purchases is the way to go. RDQ economists do not expect QE news today, but instead see the Fed saying it stands ready to provide further accommodation should the risks to recovery increase. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1227 GMT [Dow Jones] EUROPEAN SUMMARY: The USD is higher against its rivals and equities are trading lower as investors square positions ahead of the FOMC meeting later Tuesday. Recent weakness in economic data has raised concerns about the U.S. economy, but it remains to be seen if the Fed announces further policy measures or just alters its statement to highlight economic uncertainties. Earlier Tuesday, China's trade surplus widened to an 18-month high in July as exports rose 38.1% from a year ago, while the BOJ kept its policy stance unchanged despite calls for further easing to halt the JPY's rise. Over in the U.K., the trade deficit narrowed more than forecast in June as exports rose to their highest level in nearly two years. EUR/USD is down at 1.3131, GBP/USD is lower at 1.5728, and USD/JPY is little changed at 86.09. September gilts are trading 0.05 lower at 122.41 and September bunds are down 0.11 at 129.92. Nymex crude is down $1.43 at $80.05/bbl. Key European stock indexes are trading down around 0.5% but are off lows. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1221 GMT [Dow Jones] Ireland's 5-year sovereign CDS are 15 bps wider at 246 bps, in line with a weaker overall credit market, according to Markit figures. Ireland underperformed some non-core euro-zone sovereigns Monday, widening 4 bps while Spain and Portugal tightened. "There was talk around new supply ahead of today's auction announcement and also the market is starting to think about the cost to the government of supporting \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  (AIB)," a trader says. The Irish government said at the end of June it needed to give Anglo Irish a EUR8.6B capital injection to support Tier 1 capital - paid in annual installments over 10 years. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1215 GMT [Dow Jones] 7 to 30-yr Tsys are up a bit as stock futures fall, with investors cautious ahead of FOMC news. Investors on guard to see whether Fed makes any mention of more bond buying to help economy. Before that though, market has busy day. Data include productivity and wholesale trade figures. Govt will sell $34B in 3-yr notes at 1 p.m. Traders expect sale will go well as nervous investors stock up on Tsys before the Fed news. 2-yr dn 1/32 to 0.553%, 10-yr up 3/32 to 2.816% and 30-yr up 12/32 to 3.997%. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1213 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro is sliding against the USD and JPY after the BoJ announced no new policy steps and a report showed slowing demand for imports in China, which damped demand for growth-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Markets remain focused on the Fed's rate-setting meeting this afternoon for fresh signs of quantitative easing. EUR/USD at 1.3141 from 1.3225 late Mon, according to EBS via CQG, while EUR/JPY at 113.04 from 113.57. USD/JPY at 86.00 from 85.84. The ICE Dollar Index at 81.227 from 80.700.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1205 GMT [Dow Jones] Markit's CDX NAIG 14 is weaker with stock futures as investors nervously eye this afternoon's announcement from the Fed, due at 2:15 p.m. ET. The benchmark credit derivatives index is at 104.2BP, 2.17BP wider than yesterday's close, according to Markit. The CDX High Yield is down 0.35 pt at 98.29. DJIA futures off 68.(frances.mcinnis@dowjones.com) [ 08-10-10 0841ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100810e68a00082\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
