\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Polish zloty current level satisfactory-c.bank\b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2010\par 07:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Current zloty level satisfactory-c.bank head Belka\par \par \uc2 * Sharp zloty appreciation could pose threat\par \par \uc2 * Belka would like to see smaller deficit in 2011 (Adds quotes, detail)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Aug 10 (Reuters) - The level of the Polish zloty is satisfactory and if anything threatens the Polish unit, it is sharp appreciation rather than depreciation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor Marek Belka said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Belka added that he would have hoped to see a smaller budget deficit next year, but underlined that Poland did not need very deep fiscal cuts.\par \par \uc2 "If something threatens the zloty, it is its sharp appreciation rather than depreciation," Belka told daily Puls Biznesu in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "I hope (the zloty) appreciation trend won't be too strong. The current zloty level is satisfactory."\par \par \uc2 The governor also repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if such a need emerges.\par \par \uc2 The zloty <EURPLN=> has swung back and forth in recent months. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interevened to counter its rising strength in April, while there were reports in May that the finance ministry had sold euros on the market to stop its sharp fall.\par \par \uc2 It hovered around its 3-month highs early on Tuesday, and was traded at 3.98, a touch below a key level of 4.0 against the euro.\par \par \uc2 BUDGET\par \par \uc2 The governor also said he expected the government to curb next year's budget deficit more ambitiously.\par \par \uc2 "Today it is only a fall to 45 billion zlotys from 52 billion zlotys (envisaged this year)," Belka said. "I expected next year's deficit to be smaller."\par \par \uc2 Under the government's new financial plan, budget deficit is set to be trimmed gradually to 45 billion zlotys next year and to 40 billion zlotys in 2012. The key goal of the plan is to cut the general budget deficit, that includes shortfalls generated by local government's and agencies, to the EU ceiling of 3 percent in 2013.\par \par \uc2 The plan envisages a temporary increase in value-added tax (VAT) by 1 percentage points from 2011 and the governor said last week such a move is tolerable.\par \par \uc2 However, he said now he expected the government to cut spending as other countries consolidate their public finances nowadays.\par \par \uc2 "However, it doesn't have to be sharp or sudden cuts," Belka said. (Reporting by Dagmara Leszkowicz; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-ZLOTY/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|PX|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100810e68a0008u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
