\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/PLN Seen Testing 4.20 Monday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2010\par 10:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty has stabilized somewhat after its dramatic Friday weakening and is expected to test 4.20 resistance against the euro, a dealer says. 4.20 is seen as the key level at which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Ministry could potentially \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market, the dealer adds. EUR/PLN trades at 4.1819 from 4.1800 late Friday, USD/PLN at 3.5000, sharply lower from the 3.3300 level the pair hit Friday. Average yield on 2-year paper is unchanged from late Friday at 4.70%, on 5-year at 5.35% from 5.37% and on 10-year also unchanged at 5.84%. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks remain in negative territory but STI paring losses as investors nibble at battered-down blue chips. Benchmark down 1.7% at 2757.85 vs low of 2726.80. While index not expected to turn positive with U.S. stock futures weak, European bourses opening lower, it's expected to close above current June low of 2710. Defensive plays best performers among STI components, with SMRT (S53.SG) +0.9% at S$2.16, \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG) +0.3% at S$4.02, ST Engineering (S63.SG) flat at S$3.18. Broad market volume remains weak, at under 700 million shares, may end at new 2010 low; current year-to-date low at 920 million shares achieved Friday. Decliners outnumering gainers 7 to 1 in broad market.(frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0822 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds sustain gains as concerns over euro zone debt problems lead to safe haven flows into government securities, says dealer with state-run bank; still, thinks bonds will likely trim gains if government decides to hike fuel prices later today. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond last at 102.28, after touching high of 102.50 vs 101.93 Friday's close. "The yields will rise if the government decides to hike oil prices but in the long term, demand for government bonds likely to remain strong due to European debt problems, poor US jobs data and RBI's steps to ensure adequate liquidity," says dealer. Tips 2020 bond yield in 7.40%-7.50% band vs last 7.47%. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0821 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says with huge EUR/CHF stop losses taken-out Friday after the pair broke through 1.40 to lows around 1.3866, the cross should calm a little. However if EUR/USD continues to fall, EUR/CHF will remain under pressure. The bank says the SNB may step back in to smooth such moves but it must be aware that it will be difficult to stem such massive downside pressure on the euro. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  looks for the cross to remain capped by 1.40 now and says there's a chance the SNB will change its wording on the franc at its June 17 rate-setting meeting. EUR/CHF is now at 1.3896 from a fresh all-time low of 1.3853 marked in Asia Monday. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0820 GMT [Dow Jones] The latest IMM speculative positioning data shows non-commercial investors reduced their exposure to the high-beta commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notes \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 . Net long positions in AUD, NZD and CAD are now back to levels seen just after markets rebounded in March 2009 and so downside risk from position unwinding is likely to have been reduced significantly, says the bank. AUD/USD trades at 0.8133, NZD/USD at 0.6632 and USD/CAD at 1.0655. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)-o-\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT [Dow Jones] The outcome of Dutch elections June 9 is very open but typically Dutch politics does not impact Dutch government bonds, DSLs, says ING strategist Padhraic Garvey. DSLs are, in turn, supported by relatively positive Dutch fundamentals, he says. The Netherlands Tuesday sells EUR3 billion to EUR4 billion of DSL 1.75% January 2013. Separately, Credit Agricole CIB says the auction size is "quite big" for a Dutch offering, and the fear of some ingestion has been reflected by the bond's underperformance against surrounding issues. The marginally cheaper level vs surrounding DSLs may, however, make the auction a little easier to stomach, it says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0817 GMT [Dow Jones] The risk of an intermediate rate hike from the Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is clearly on the rise, \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  says post comments from the new Hungarian government that rattled investor nerves Friday. The key policy rate is at 5.25% after several months of rate cuts. Danske sees a clear risk of more market turmoil in the coming days if the Hungarian government doesn't act decisively. Adds there's already been contagion from Hungary to other European markets, the next countries endangered are Romania and Bulgaria. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] It "is not easy to explain" the underperformance of Italian government bonds, BTPs, as there are no rating concerns and the government has recently announced new fiscal measures to contain the deficit, says Luca Cazzulani, strategist at \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  in Milan. "Most likely, BTPs have been under pressure due to a lack of support from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond purchase program as well as from a general resurgence of EMU [euro zone] woes," he says. The 10-year BTP-Bund spread is currently 1.72 percentage points versus 1.69 percentage points at Friday's close. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0808 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 2.0% at 16,777.25, tracking sharp losses in regional markets on concerns euro zone debt problems worsening; "Right now, some problems have cropped up in Hungary and it is expected that more European countries may fall into this (debt) trap," says R.K. Gupta, managing director of Taurus Asset Management. Thinks investors will switch focus to domestic newsflow from late July as corporates will begin reporting 1Q results, while progress of monsoon can be better assessed. "Monsoons are very crucial for the Indian economy particularly after the failure of the previous monsoons," he adds. Tips strong support for Sensex at 16,500. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) down 4% at INR465.25, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) off 3.1% at INR839.90, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) down 2.1% at INR1,009, \uc2 Bharat Heavy Electricals\uc2  (500103.BY) off 2.4% at INR2,276. S&P CNX Nifty down 2.0% at 5030.95. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0807 GMT [Dow Jones] Tumbling equities are encouraging another shift into safe havens, to the benefit of the USD, JPY and, to some extent, the CHF. The EUR is falling towards $1.19 despite Hungary's reassurance it is not facing a Greek-style crisis. A lack of official concern for its sharp decline is only making matters worse. The USD is down at Y91.45, while the EUR is down at $1.1926. The GBP is down at $1.4429. The EUR is trading down at CHF1.3898, but there is still no sign of SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end up amid weak stocks, fading liquidity squeeze after Bank of China's massive convertible bond sale ended last week. Shanghai exchange government bond index up ends at 125.46 vs 125.31 last close. "Investors now focus on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lower the yield on the one-year bills to be sold in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  tomorrow as liquidity pressure eases," says Beijing-based trader with local fund. PBOC raised 1-year bill yield by 8bps to 2.0096% last week due to tight liquidity caused by BOC's bond sale. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] Hong Kong GDP growth may have peaked in 1Q, is likely to ease in coming quarters tracking lingering concern over euro zone sovereign debt crisis, says Hang Seng Bank. Says HSI has lost about 11% since onset of debt crisis in mid-April, while negative wealth effect from weaker stocks may hurt consumer confidence, spending. Adds weakening EUR, GBP will also hurt Asia's export prospects as European Union is important market, accounting for about 20% of China's exports, about 13% of Hong Kong's. Expects Hong Kong GDP to decelerate sharply from +8.2% on year in 1Q to +3.0% in 4Q, with average +5.0% for 2010. (chester.yung@dowjones.com) [ 06-07-10 0424ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100607e66700064\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
