\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Polish zloty at good rate-Deputy PM\b0\par\par\uc2 329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2010\par 10:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Zloty good for econ when touch above 4 euro-Deputy PM\par \par \uc2 * Polish Dep PM says rate hikes should be avoided\par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, detail)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, May 20 (Reuters) - Poland's zloty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has weakened due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is now at a competitive rate for the country's economy, Deputy Prime Minister and Economy Minister Waldemar Pawlak said on Thursday. "The zloty exchange rate at a touch above 4 (versus the euro) is a very good exchange rate that improves our competitiveness, " Pawlak, who is the presidential candidate for the junior governing coalition Peasant Party, told reporters.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April to help weaken the zloty <EURPLN=D3> when it traded at about 3.84 against the euro. It now trades at about 4.12.\par \par \uc2 "The market indicates the zloty at 4 zlotys versus euro is the equilibrium rate," Pawlak said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April, supported by the finance ministry, was the first such move since Poland introduced a fully free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2000.\par \par \uc2 Pawlak also said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should try to avoid interest rate increases.\par \par \uc2 "We have interest rates at 3.5 percent and much above the levels in Europe and the United States and slightly above inflation and we should not turn the screw further as it would hurt economic growth," Pawlak said.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike rates by 25 basis points towards the end of the year because inflation is expected to start accelerating some time in autumn.\par \par \uc2 Poland's economy is also seen growing by about 3 percent this year, up from 1.8 percent in 2009, when Poland was the only European Union economy to have avoided recession thanks to its large internal market and zloty weakening last year. (Reporting by Agata Nalecz, writing by Karolina Slowikowska; editing by Jason Webb)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-ZLOTY/ (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|RNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100520e65k000uy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
