\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Poland \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bank May  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2 , Cut Rates - ING\b0\par\par\uc2 1,532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2010\par 09:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0719 GMT [Dow Jones] If EUR/PLN returns below 3.84, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  first, then will consider rate cuts, ING says. ING comments on the Monetary Policy Council minutes released Thursday, showing the MPC considered rate cut on its March meeting. The banks adds the MPC will consider rate cuts only if slow growth, headline CPI and credit action warrant it. "The money market has already delivered one rate cut by means of a 25 basis point drop in the Wibor," ING says. "We see limited room for further reductions of 3-6 month funding rates." (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY sharply up in onshore spot market at 6.8298 vs 6.8263 last close, around 6.8265 midday on import settlements while shortage of USD in interbank market raises demand for greenback, trader at foreign bank says; "there are various reasons for the strong dollar bids. One of them is that importers could be rushing to buy dollars as the month-end nears," she says. Increase in USD loans so far this year led to shortage of USD supply among local banks; strong bids in spot market could easily cause USD short squeeze, trader says. USD/CNY may test immediate resistance at around 6.8300. Still, market participants say expectations of CNY rise remains. Offshore 1-year NDFs at 6.6040/6.6065 vs 6.6000/6.6050 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] Once again, it is time to support the JPY, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus by Nicholas Hastings. Despite clear signs last month that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will come under selling pressure if, and when, investors focus on yields, there is little sign of this happening yet. In fact, developments over the last few days suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to be driven largely by risk and that, as a safe haven, the JPY will continue to benefit. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD still lower at 31.359 vs 31.398 late yesterday on exporters' selling to hedge \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, foreign funds inflows into local shares (Taiex ends +0.3%) with foreign investors buying net NT$3.99 billion of Taiwan shares, says local bank trader. "The pair may fall further in the near term on expectations major tech firms will deliver better-than-expected earnings figures next week," he says. Still, expects decline to be partially offset by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's last-minute buying to stem TWD rise, pushing pair near 31.370 level. Tips USD/TWD in 31.350-31.370 band in final hour of Taipei trade. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.6% at 21,331.93, led by falls in property stocks, with index in third day of losses likely to test psychological 21,000 level near-term, says Tanrich's Jackson Wong. Suggests there's no rush to buy stocks now, as "we should never catch a falling knife." Further falls in property stocks expected, given concerns of further tightening ahead in both HK, China. Hang Seng Property Subindex down 1.6% at 26,373.80. Among these, China Overseas (0688.HK) down 3.3% at HK$14.62, New World Development (0017.HK) down 3.0% at HK$14.14. \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK) +0.2% at HK$16.72 after it says it may be immune to penalty from U.K. price-fixing case. Volume modest at HK$42.312 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] Volume in Singapore bourse likely to end below 2 billion shares for second straight day as participation so far remains subdued, with investors content to wait on sidelines for clearer market direction. 1.14 billion shares changed hands so far vs yesterday's 1.71 billion, with small caps most active. Interest in blue chips thin, with STI off 0.3% at 2971.72, still locked in tight 2968-2982 band since morning. Any further downside may find support at this week's low of 2943, while resistance remains at 3000, which index hasn't tested this whole week. "The market is going to end on a low note. Nothing much is happening and people are just happy to stay out for now," says trader at local brokerage. Declines by STI components led by OCBC (O39.SG), off 1.8% at S$8.77, \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), off 1.3% at S$3.08, ComfortDelgro (C52.SG), off 1.9% at S$1.59. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0703 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.1% at 1335.52 in lackluster volume of 470 million shares; dealer tips index in 1330-1340 range for rest of day in absence of fresh leads. Says local sentiment weak on concerns global economic recovery could be derailed by Greece debt problems. Still, tech stocks shine today; "the recent strong earnings by U.S. technology firms are providing a boost for local chipmakers"; sub-index last +1.7%. Market breadth negative with 303 decliners vs 219 advancers. Decliners include Pos Malaysia (4634.KU), down 3.7% at MYR2.90, \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU), down 1.2% at MYR8.79, MAS (3786.KU), down 2.2% at MYR2.21. Among gainers, MISC (3816.KU) +2.2% at MYR8.99, Unisem (5005.KU) +4.2% at MYR3.45, Globetronics (7022.KU) +6.3% at MYR1.69. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end mostly lower after Commercial Times reports OTC Chairman Gordon Chen suggested setting limits for secondary market trading similar to primary market, where single investor can't buy more than a third of bond auctioned by finance ministry. Still, traders say such restriction likely won't be implemented because of conflicting interests of government agencies. "This is highly unlikely. The OTC wants volume but the finance ministry wants low yields," says local brokerage trader. Local bank trader says, "if it was so easy (to do), it would have been done a long time ago." Adds, players keeping eye on Wednesday auction of NT$40 billion reopened 20-year government bonds; thinks insurers likely to buy around 2.0%. Reopened 20-year bond yield ends up at 1.9775% vs 1.9753% in when-issued trade. Yield on most-active 5-year bond ends up at 1.0023% vs 0.9878%, tipped in 0.99%-1.02% band next week. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] June Bunds are unchanged on the day at 124.01 after hitting a contract high Thursday. Sean Maloney at Nomura says bunds are continuing to probe the upside with the recent two-month top at 123.88 giving way and reaching as high as 124.31. "This leaves February's peak of 124.53 well in sight, a break of which would fuel bullish impetus and a likely push to 125.00," he says. Support tipped at 123.62, 123.25, and 122.88. Resistance at 124.31, 124.53, and 125.00. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Australia's 1Q10 inflation likely to rise vs 4Q09; Dow Jones survey of 20 economists points to inflation of 0.8% in 1Q from 4Q's 0.5%. Rising prices for vegetables, annual education fees, electricity, gasoline will contribute to higher prices. Annual inflation likely to grow from 2.1% to 2.8%, core inflation likely around 0.7% in the quarter, or 3.0% over the year. Results like these will leave RBA with little room for policy flexibility. Any upside surprises will see bets for further rate hike in May increase. Data due Wednesday 0130 GMT. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia CDS spread widening, fanned by Greece's fiscal troubles, but not expected to last given region's positive economic fundamentals, say analysts and traders. USD bond, CDS spreads broadly wider to flat, but that's just "to allow for some consolidation in a relatively hectic week for trading, primary deals and liability management exercises," says Credit Agricole analyst Brayan Lai. Markit iTraxx Asia ex-Japan investment-grade CDS index around 3 bps wider after ending at 96 bps yesterday. Other single-name sovereign CDS also around 3-4bps wider. Indonesia, Philippine 5-year CDS flat, in plus or minus 1-2bps range. (ditas.lopez@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece does have serious problems, and the path of austerity adjustment is going to be a long and painful one but their announced measures aren't currently being given a chance to take effect, says \uc2 Nomura International\uc2 . "Until that happens, it is hard to evaluate the validity of current market moves with any sort of conviction," Nomura says. Greek 10-year yield spread over German bunds trade at 5.66% at 0639 GMT, compared with Thursday's close at 5.74%. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com) [ 04-23-10 0319ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100423e64n0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
