\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3-Polish March wages up 4.8 y/y, above forecast\b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2010\par 14:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  WARSAW, April 19 (Reuters) - Polish corporate sector wages grew faster than expected in March while the pace of decline in corporate employment slowed, data showed on Monday, cementing expectations for a steady economic recovery.\par \par \uc2  Polish corporate sector wages <PLWAGE=ECI> rose 4.8 percent year-on-year in March to 3,493 zlotys per month, up from a 2.9 percent rise in February, the statistics office data showed.\par \par \uc2  In monthly terms, wages rose by 6.2 percent.\par \par \uc2  Data also showed corporate employment <PLEMPY=ECI> fell 0.6 percent year-on-year in March and was unchanged in monthly terms.\par \par \uc2  Analysts said both the wage and employment data boded well for the labour market, consumption and economic growth but said they did not expect any impact on this month's meeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Council (MPC).\par \par \uc2  The MPC is expected to keep the main interest rate flat at 3.5 percent, though some analysts said Monday's better-than- expected data bolstered expectations for monetary tightening in the second half of 2010 to offset increased demand.\par \par \uc2  Consumer price inflation in March stood at 2.6 percent and is expected to decline further in coming months before going into reverse as the recovery accelerates.  ***********************************************************\par \par \uc2  KEY POINTS  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  WAGES (pct change)        March 2010  Feb 2010  March 2009\par  Year-on-year                 4.8        +2.9        5.7\par  CORPORATE EMPLOYMENT  \par  Year-on-year (pct change)   -0.6        -1.1       -0.9\par  \par  FORECAST:  \par  * Analysts in a Reuters poll <ECONPL> had forecast wages to\par have grown 2.9 percent year-on-year in March.  \par  * Analysts in a Reuters poll had forecast employment to have\par fallen 0.7 percent year-on-year in March.\par  \par  MARKET REACTION:  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  The Polish zloty <EURPLN=D3> <PLN=> and bonds were little\par changed immediately after the data. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  ANALYSTS' COMMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  ARKADIUSZ KRZESNIAK, CHIEF ECONOMIST AT DEUTSCHE BANK IN\par WARSAW\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "In terms of wages, the data is better than expected.\par Employment data too. The data suggests the situation in the\par corporate sector is improving.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "The acceleration is significant, but we had quite a lot of\par volatility in recent months. I think the real growth of wages\par will persist in coming months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "The data itself will have no impact on the Monetary Policy\par Council."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  ELISABETH ANDREEW, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STRATEGIST AT NORDEA:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "The data is better than expected and supports our view that\par there are some early signs that the weakest point in terms of\par the unemployment rate is approaching.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "Business surveys, for example, show a significant\par improvement in the balance between businesses expecting to\par increase employment and businesses expecting to reduce\par employment, though the gap remains slightly in negative\par territory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "All in all, the PLN is likely to be unaffected as the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems committed to taking the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate into\par consideration in its policy, i.e. risk of postponed rate hikes\par and/or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "The PLN is also hit by the generally weaker risk sentiment\par today and uncertainty about the presidential elections."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  PETER ATTARD MONTALTO, ECONOMIST AT NOMURA IN LONDON:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "Poland wages surprised strongly to the upside as the\par economy bounced back from its soft spot and the labour market\par shows signs of turning too.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "The print is particularly important in our view for the\par risk to CPI and supports our view of inflation turning around\par and moving back up during the second half of this year,\par prompting rate hikes at that time (we pencil in 3x25bp) with\par demand pressures starting to become evident. It should also be\par remembered that the MPC does have a soft spot for labour data."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  GRZEGORZ MALISZEWSKI, CHIEF ECONOMIST AT MILLENNIUM BANK:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "Maybe the payout of bonuses was moved to March (and that is\par why the data was better than expected). We have to wait for more\par detailed data which will show whether this was not a one-off,\par which is also possible.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  "It seems, however, that the employment data was more\par important because they show that the situation on the labour\par market is stabilising. And this is positive."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  BACKGROUND:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may still need \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - finmin\par [ID:nLDE63D17C]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * Polish inflation eases to 2.6 perent in March\par [ID:nLDE63E0L]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * Polish Jan-March budget gap at 43.5 pct of 2010 target\par [ID:nWSF008945]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2  * Polish Feb C/A swings into surplus [ID:nLDE63C0KM]\par   (Writing by Karolina Slowikowska)\par \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 POLAND-WAGES/ (INSTANT VIEW 3)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|PX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100422e64j0015f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\