\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-PREVIEW-Polish CPI seen easing to 30-month low in March\b0\par\par\uc2 508 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2010\par 13:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * WHAT: March inflation\par \par \uc2 * WHEN: Thursday, April 15, at 1200 GMT\par \par \uc2 REUTERS FORECAST: March inflation +2.6 pct y/y, +0.4 pct m/m. For full Polish April macropoll, click on [ID:nLDE6381L5].\par \par \uc2 FACTORS TO WATCH:\par \par \uc2 Inflation is expected to ease to just above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2.5 percent target and reach its lowest level since September 2007 mostly thanks to statistical base effects and a stronger zloty.\par \par \uc2 The rise of regulated prices was far less this year than in 2009 and the zloty strength in 2010 makes imports less expensive, which will be now reflected in lower year-on-year inflation indicators.\par \par \uc2 Price growth is expected to abate in the coming months and inflation should reach its lowest level in the summer at about 2 percent, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Inflation has been easing since the beginning of the year with the February reading of 2.9 percent coming well below 3.5 percent recorded for January.\par \par \uc2 Despite the expected easing in price growth dynamics, analysts polled by Reuters still expect the Monetary Policy Council to raise interest rates by a total of 50 basis points before the end of 2010.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Slawomir Skrzypek, who was also an MPC member, perished in a plane crash on Saturday, but most analysts said this would not change the short-term interest rate outlook.\par \par \uc2 The main interest rate currently stands at an all-time low of 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION: A lower reading could help improve bond market sentiment, particularly on shorter-dated two-year bonds, which are the most sensitive to indications of future monetary policy. The zloty <EURPLN=>, <PLN=> is often supported by bond market gains.\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2 Poland's inflation is highly sensitive to the exchange-rate. Depreciation can quickly increase price growth, while appreciation does the opposite.\par \par \uc2 Poland's zloty has been rallying in recent weeks, reaching a 16-month high against the euro and raising concern among central bankers that zloty gains could harm the recovery.\par \par \uc2 Friday, for the first time since \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted the free-float in 2000, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the zloty.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost about 1 percent against the euro but analysts remained sceptical about the longevity of the fall. Poland's strong economic fundamentals have pushed the zloty up about 6 percent against the euro since the start of 2010.\par \par \uc2 The zloty's gains could help cap inflationary pressures and possibly cool monetary policy tightening expectations.\par \par \uc2 Poland was the only economy in the European Union to avoid recession last year, thanks to a large domestic consumer base and the boost Polish exporters got from a weaker zloty.\par \par \uc2 Growth is now seen at 2.9 percent in 2010, up from 1.7 percent in 2009. For comparison, the economy grew by 5 percent in 2008. (Writing by Kuba Jaworowski; Editing by Neil Stempleman)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-INFLATION/ (PREVIEW)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|PX|RNP|DNP    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100415e64f000yu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
