\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Stocks rise 1 pct; Dubai's DEWA to sell bond\b0\par\par\uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2010\par 13:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks jump 1 pct; just off recent 19-1/2 mth peaks\par \par \uc2 * Lira rises as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unveils exit policies\par \par \uc2 * Dubai's DEWA sets price guidance for dollar bond\par \par \uc2 By Sujata Rao\par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 14 (Reuters) - Emerging stocks rose 1 percent on Wednesday as strong corporate results boosted global equities, while a state-run Dubai entity prepared to sell the first bond from the emirate since last year's shock debt freeze.\par \par \uc2 Forecast-beating results by chipmaker Intel and investment bank \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  as well as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation in Singapore that re-ignited expectations of a move in the yuan were catalysts for emerging market gains.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark MSCI emerging equity index <.MSCIEF> tracked gains on core markets to rise back close to recent 19-1/2 month highs and exchanges in Istanbul <.XU100>, Johannesburg <.JTOPI> and Moscow <.IRTS> rallied almost one percent.\par \par \uc2 Emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were moderately higher as the dollar slipped, with the Turkish lira outperforming <TRY=> amid expectations for a rate increase by the second half of 2010.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been supported by a revaluation in Singapore dollar <SGD=> which hit a 20-month high, indicating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's shift in focus from growth to inflation. Asia's tech-heavy bourses such as Korea and Taiwan were also lifted by the Intel results.\par \par \uc2 "Asia was first off the block and that fed through (into emerging Europe)," said Nigel Rendell, emerging markets strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We are trading one day to the next and valuations, whether on bonds or stocks, are quite expensive but there are quite a few supportive elements so the rally may continue a bit longer."\par \par \uc2 He identified market-positive Hungarian election results and the Turkish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to a slightly less dovish stance as supportive for forint and lira -- both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are up 1.5 percent this week.\par \par \uc2 The forint neared a four-week high to the euro <EURHUF=>. while the lira hit a 2-1/2-month high versus the dollar <TRY=>.\par \par \uc2 Rendell said the Singapore move highlights the theme of rate normalisation across emerging markets, starting with Asia, even though most central banks have been nervous about increasing interest rates to avoid disrupting economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 TURKEY\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for instance announced an end to some stimulus measures introduced during the crisis but stressed the exit would be "cautious and gradual". Despite rising inflation, it signalled this week rates would stay low for a while.\par \par \uc2 Turkish bonds' response was muted while stocks extended gains but analysts say the liquidity withdrawal would eventually hit bonds which are overwhelmingly held by local banks.\par \par \uc2 "Exit policies, combined with gradually improving prospects for lending....should over time start to weigh on the local bond market. In contrast, these measures are unlikely to curb the recent trend for lira strengthening," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  told clients.\par \par \uc2 Also in emerging Europe, the Czech crown hit a seven-month high <EURCZK=> while the zloty firmed 0.13 percent <EURPLN=>.\par \par \uc2 Traders said crown gains were not due to Czech-specific factors. Some cited the closure of long zloty/crown trades after \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last week.\par \par \uc2 "Everything is fairly strong as nothing has happened to shake the mood. Obviously in Poland, you have to watch for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  if we get up to 3.85 again," one trader said.\par \par \uc2 "I expect there will be another attempt soon, everything points to a stronger zloty."\par \par \uc2 Bond markets also were demonstrating the strong appetite for emerging market risk -- Turkey's 1.5 billion-euro 2020 issue launched on Tuesday garnered orders of almost 6 billion euros while a raft of quasi-sovereigns are marketing bonds.\par \par \uc2 Dubai's state-run utility DEWA is expected on Wednesday to price a dollar bond, with price guidance at 8.75 percent but sources said the issue has already traded almost a point higher in the grey market. This is the first issue since Dubai called a standstill on billions of dollars of debt last November.\par \par \uc2 The deal was said to have received orders of about $4 billion by the time books closed in London.\par \par \uc2 "The price talk is 8.75 percent which is extremely generous. That's a spread of 615 basis points over U.S. Treasuries," one London-based banker said.\par \par \uc2 He added the issue also offered a yield pickup of 125 bps over Dubai though Dewa tends to trade flat to the sovereign.\par \par \uc2 Another deal expected this week is state-run Ukrainian bank Ukreximbank with a five-year dollar issue. That would be the first from the ex-Soviet country following the restructuring of another Ukrainian quasi-sovereign Naftogaz last year and may pave the way for a sovereign bond later this year.\par \par \uc2 Russia is marketing its first bond in a decade and Montenegro said on Wednesday it plans an issue in May or June.\par \par \uc2 Sovereign emerging bond yield spreads over U.S. Treasuries fell 4 bps to 240 bps <11EMJ>.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Toby Chopra)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100417e64e0007j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
