\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: PLN A Tad Stronger, Follows Global Moods\b0\par\par\uc2 1,285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2010\par 10:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0832 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish zloty is slightly stronger following better global moods on stock markets after better than expected Intel earnings, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Millennium\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. The bank expects the market to stay calm due to fears that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again. Forecasts EUR/PLN to trade within 3.8000-3.8500 range. EUR/PLN now trades at 3.8673 from 3.8763 late Tuesday, USD/PLN at 2.8369. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0830 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 13 traded at 84, 85, 86 bps, down from yesterday's 90 bps as upturn in Tokyo shares add to brighter outlook for nation's export-reliant economy, encouraging investors to sell protection, says \uc2 Shinsei Securities\uc2  senior analyst Yasuhiro Matsumoto; Nikkei closes +0.4%. Wednesday's single-name CDS deals include \uc2 Takefuji Corp\uc2 . (8564.TO) upfront deal through May 2011 at 41.5% vs yesterday's upfront deal through July 2010 at 21.0%. Recommends holding CDS of consumer loan firms ahead of their earnings releases; Promise Co. (8574.TO) on May 10; Takefuji Cop., \uc2 Acom Co\uc2 . (8572.TO) on May 13. "While the CDS market will likely become subdued (toward earnings season), the index for the consumer loan sector may hover at high levels" amid their sluggish financing environment. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0829 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are off day's lows with markets still not convinced that the troubles in Greece are over. Glenn Marci at \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  says there are still doubts about how the Greek aid package will work, especially the impact on countries like Italy, which have shaky finances but are expected to pick up a share of the tab. June bunds are now unchanged on the day at 122.98, having fallen to 122.78 earlier Wednesday. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0821 GMT [Dow Jones] Finland will auction two government bonds simultaneously April 20, and \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects the 4% July 2025 bond to bear the brunt of the up to EUR1.5 billion supply, says strategist David Schnautz. Year-to-date Finland has sold EUR8 billion of government bonds out of the EUR16 billion to EUR17 billion planned for 2010, he calculates. Assuming Finland sells a further EUR1.5 billion at another auction in 2Q, funding at the end of 1H should be in the ballpark of EUR11 billion or close to two thirds of total Finnish bond supply in 2010, Schautz says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0817 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds steady late, with yields not moving more than 1-2 bps either way, says dealer with local bank-backed brokerage. "There are few fresh drivers...trading has been lackluster," dealer says, likely to continue tomorrow. Adds, weak demand in auction of new 20-year benchmark "a bit of a surprise," but notes that demand for such long tenure bonds hard to forecast as investor base "tends to be very niche...there may not be an urgent requirement." Yields for September 2017 MGS down 1 bp at 3.90%, November 2019 MGS +1 bp at 4.09%, August 2015 MGS flat at 3.63%, May 2013 MGS +1 bp at 3.19%. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece is on track to meet its 2010 deficit goals based on 1Q data, Credit Agricole says, but warns it may still seek EU/IMF aid. Says: "EU/IMF package should largely solve the problems of the Greeks being able to find enough cash to pay their creditors. Maybe Greece can get through without calling upon the EU/IMF reserve, perhaps they will not." But notes 40% decline in the Q1 budget deficit and adds: "If this is continued for the year, then the budget deficit will reach 7.8%. Officially, the government is targeting an improvement to 8.7%. So far, so good." (alkman.granitsas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0806 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD is down, except against the JPY, as strong 1Q growth in Singapore and good earnings from Intel improve general market sentiment to the cost of safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD is up at Y93.43 while the EUR is up at $1.3628. Strong U.S. retail sales later in the day could boost sentiment even further. However, data showing slower industrial production growth in the euro zone could stop the EUR from rising much more. The GBP is up at $1.5410. nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0759 GMT [Dow Jones] With US unemployment remaining high, the housing market still not recovering, and with the troubled commercial real estate area remaining deflationary, Fed Chairman Bernanke is hardly worried about inflation at this stage. Also, he is hardly likely to shift guidance unilaterally ahead of an FOMC in about two weeks time. Nevertheless, says Tullett Prebon, Bernanke's comments to Congress later in the day could still "create some jitters" as he will be keen to discourage complacency among investors and might prove less dovish than the market expects. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0758 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are lower Wednesday as rising stocks damp demand for fixed income securities and traders lighten positions following Tuesday's auction. Strategist at European bank says the prospects of a hung parliament will ensure trading is volatile till the elections due on May 6. With no UK data of note on Wednesday, Fed Chairman Bernanke's testimony at 1400 GMT will be closely eyed along with U.S. retail sales and CPI data at 1230 GMT. The 2s/10s gild yield curve steepens to 310.4bps vs 307.8 on Tuesday. The June gilt contract is down 25bps at 113.82 and the benchmark 10-year gilt is down at 97.78 to yield 4.036%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0758 GMT [Dow Jones] Assuming Greece uses its EU/IMF package, the cost will be spread among other euro-zone members, and that's bad news for other under-stress nations like Ireland and Spain, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Both of those countries stand to pump around 0.3% of their already-stretched GDP into the pot, the bank says. Watch out for any impact on these countries' CDS, and be warned that "any further scrutiny of other countries will keep the euro under pressure." EUR/USD well supported by broadly buoyant sentiment Wednesday at 1.3627. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] As dust from the Greek note auction settles, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  sums up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's position: "The EUR is caught between a rock and a hard place." The bank reckons that it will be some time before the EUR can escape from this position as it is suffering from fears that a Greek default may just have been postponed and that there is still the risk of 'moral hazard' with other euro zone debtors. nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0746 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS lower as March CPI likely falls from last month. 1-year down 2 bps at 2.18%-2.24%, 5-year down 4 bps at 3.54%-3.62%, 1-to-5-year down 2 bps at 137 bps. "The possible slower inflation growth rate triggered concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably won't hike interest rates in the second quarter." March CPI likely +2.6% on year, tad slower than February's 2.7% rise, according to economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires. China will release March CPI, 1Q GDP at 0200 GMT tomorrow. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) [ 04-14-10 0432ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100414e64e0005s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*