\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 2-Polish Feb C/A posts 106 mln euro surplus\b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2010\par 14:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW, April 13 (Reuters) - Poland's current account swung into a surplus of 106 million euros in February from a revised deficit of 754 million euros in January, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Tuesday, largely thanks to an inflow of EU funds.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters had expected a deficit of 203 million euros.\par \par \uc2 In other data, Poland's trade deficit stood at 248 million euros in the second month of 2010.\par \par \uc2 The total current account gap in 2009 stood at 4,968 million euros compared with 18,293 million a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Analysts attributed the improvement to transfers of European Union funds to Poland and said they expected the current account balance to deteriorate again as the recovery from a sharp slowdown gathers pace.\par \par \uc2 In a bid to improve the quality of the data the bank initiated a new way of gathering it as of Jan. 1, 2010, but analysts still have doubts over its reliability. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   ***********************************************************\par  (millions of euros; * denotes revision)  \par                        February 2010   January 2010  \par  CURRENT ACCOUNT              +106            -754*      \par  Trade balance                -248            -205*    \par  exports                     9,140           8,264*      \par  imports                     9,388           8,469*      \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FORECAST:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Analysts in a Reuters poll <PLPOLL1> <ECONPL> had forecast a current account deficit of 203 million euros in February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MARKET REACTION:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Polish zloty <EURPLN=> <PLN=> and bonds were unchanged immediately after the data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ANALYSTS' VIEWS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PIOTR BUJAK, SENIOR ECONOMIST AT BANK ZACHODNI WBK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "From the perspective of foreign demand, it's in line with information coming from abroad which points to improved sentiment among companies.... This is especially true for our main trading partner Germany.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The question arises whether the appreciation of the zloty won't slow exports. For now, considering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data, the zloty rate is still above the level guaranteeing the profitability of foreign trade. So we should expect the continuation of good results from exports in the coming months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "These figure are good for the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , supporting the view that the economy remains on a recovery track, while maintaining external balance."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JAROSLAW JANECKI, CHIEF ECONOMIST AT \f28 \uc2 \f2 SOCIETE GENERALE\f28 \uc2 \f2  IN WARSAW\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The figures were better than expected. This was certainly helped by a better situation in the balance of current transfers, which can be explained by an inflow of EU funds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "These figures may be mostly neutral for the market because after every quarter there is a significant revision. The surplus we're seeing may be changed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the coming months we could see the effect of the stronger zloty on exports. In addition, another factor will be demand from our main trading partners."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MARCIN MAZUREK, ANALYST AT BRE BANK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The surplus is the result of high current transfers balance due to a 1.5 billion payout from the European Union.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As for other components they recorded deficits. The trade deficit is in line with expectations. We have a significant acceleration in both exports and imports, which was to be expected seeing industrial output and retail growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We expect the current account to deteriorate later this year, a natural process for an economy coming out from recession."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BACKGROUND:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * TABLE - Polish macroeconomic forecasts for April [ID:nLDE6381L5]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb zloty, sets precedent [ID:nLDE63816K]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * OECS sees Polish growth accelerating, urges reforms [ID:nLDE6370NV]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Polish c.bank keeps rate flat, signals recovery worries [ID:nLDE62U144]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Compiled by Dagmara Leszkowicz)\f28 \par \par \uc2 POLAND-CURRENTACCOUNT/ (INSTANT VIEW 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|PX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100416e64d000yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
